<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch2-circuit-laws">
	<title>Circuit Laws</title>

	<introduction>

    	<p>Circuit laws enable us to fill up our <q>toolbox</q> with tools that enable us to simplify and analyze circuits. First, we must understand the terminology used in circuits.</p>

	</introduction>

	<section xml:id="ch2-definitions">
    	<title>Definitions</title>

		<p>A <term>circuit</term> is an interconnection of elements. It can be simple (consisting of very few parts), very complicated, or anywhere in between. Circuit elements consist of sources, passive elements, and active elements. Sources were described in <xref ref="ch1-powersources" />. They can be dependent or independent and can source either voltage or current. <term>Passive circuit elements</term> consist of components such as resistors, capacitors, inductors, and diodes. These circuit elements do not require a connection to a power supply in order to function. A resistor inherently has resistance, which does not need to be activated with a power supply. <term>Active circuit elements</term>, on the other hand, require power to operate. Transistors and operational amplifiers are examples of active circuit elements.</p>

		<p>A <term>node</term> is a point in a circuit where two or more elements are connected. It is important to recognize and understand nodes, as they are used in many of the circuit analysis tools explained in this chapter. A diagram depicting three types of circuit nodes (depicted with thick black dots) is shown in <xref ref="cktnodes" />. The rectangles in each diagram represents a <q>generic</q> circuit element (which could be a resistor, a capacitor, a source, or something else).</p>

		<figure xml:id="cktnodes">
  			<caption>Three examples of circuit nodes.</caption>
  			<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-cktnodes">
    			<shortdescription>Left: two unknown circuit elements are connected with a wire between them. A thick dot is placed at the connection. It's labeled "simple node." Middle: Three unknown circuit elements connect together in a "T." A thick dot is placed at the "T." It's labeled "node." Right: Four circuit elements are connected together in a "+." A thick dot is placed at the center of the "+." It's labeled "node."</shortdescription>
				<latex-image >
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
						\draw (0,0) to [generic, -*] ++(right:2) node[above=0.25cm] {simple node};
						\draw (2,0) to [generic] ++(right:2);
						\draw (5,0) to [generic, -*] ++(right:2) node[above=0.25cm] {node};
						\draw (7,0) to [generic] ++(right:2);
						\draw (7,0) to [generic] ++(down:2);
						\draw (10,0) to [generic, -*] ++(right:2) node[above right=0.25cm] {node};
						\draw (12,0) to [generic] ++(right:2);
						\draw (12,0) to [generic] ++(down:2);
						\draw (12,0) to [generic] ++(up:2);
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
  			</image>
		</figure>

		<p>It is important to note that a node is not always depicted with a thick black dot on a circuit diagram. Any point of common potential represents a circuit node.</p>

		<example>
			<title>Identifying nodes in a circuit</title>

			<statement>
				<p>The circuit shown in <xref ref="threenodes" /> has three nodes labeled A, B, and C. Node A is a point of common potential between the power supply and element 1. Node B is a point of common potential connecting elements 1, 2, and 3. This node looks like the nodes shown in <xref ref="cktnodes" />, as it is depicted with a thick dot (indicating an electrical connection). Finally, node C is a point of common potential connecting the low potential end of the power supply, one end of element 2, and one end of element 3.</p>

				<figure xml:id="threenodes">
  					<caption>Each of the three nodes in this circuit is shown with thick lines. Node A (left) connects the power supply and element 1. Node B (middle) connects elements 1, 2, and 3. Node C (right) connects the power supply to elements 2 and 3.</caption>
  					<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-threenodes">
    					<shortdescription>A circuit containing a DC voltage source and three unknown circuit elements. The circuit has three nodes. The high potential end of the voltage source and one of the unknown circuit elements connect at node a. The three unknown circuit elements connect at node b. Two of the unknown circuit elements and the low potential end of the voltage source connect node c. Left: node A is highlighted on the diagram with a thick line between the DC voltage source and the element it connects to and is labeled "node A." Middle: node B is highlighted on the diagram with a thick line between where the three unknown elements connect. It's labeled "node b." Right: node C is highlighted on the diagram with a thick line between the voltage source and the two unknown elements it connects to. It's labeled "node c."</shortdescription>
						<latex-image >
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\foreach \x in {0, 5, 10} {
									\draw (\x,2) to [V] (\x,0);
									\draw[] (\x,2) to [generic, l_=1] (\x+2,2);
									\draw (\x+2,2) to [generic, l_=2, *-*] (\x+2,0);
									\draw (\x+2,2) to [generic, l_=3] (\x+4,2);
									\draw (\x,0) to [short] (\x+4,0);
									\draw (\x+4,0) to [short] (\x+4,2);
								}
								\draw[very thick] (0,1.42) -- (0,2) node[above, align=center] {node \\ A} -- (0.42,2);		
								\draw[very thick] (6.58,2) -- (7.42,2);
								\draw[very thick] (7,2) node[above, align=center] {node \\ B} -- (7,1.58);
								\draw[very thick] (10,0) -- (14,0) -- (14,2) node[above, align=center] {node \\ C};
								\draw[very thick] (10,0) -- (10,0.58);
								\draw[very thick] (12,0) -- (12,0.42);
								\draw[very thick] (13.58,2) -- (14,2);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>In <xref ref="threenodes" />, we can say that elements 2 and 3 share two common nodes (nodes B and C). Later in this chapter we will learn that devices that are connected with two common nodes are said to be in parallel with each other.</p>
			</statement>
		</example>

		<p>A <term>branch</term> in a circuit represents a portion of a circuit that exists in a path between two nodes. A branch is a path of common current. A <term>loop</term> is a closed path that starts at a node and returns to the same node. Finally, a <term>mesh</term> is a loop that has no other loops inside of it. Nodes, loops and meshes are important concepts, as they are used in circuit analysis tools explained later in this chapter.</p>

		<example>
			<title>Identifying branches, loops, and meshes in a circuit</title>

			<statement>
				<p>The circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="cktloopmesh" /> has six branches: one containing each circuit element (sinusoidal source, DC voltage source, DC current source, resistor, capacitor, and inductor).</p>

				<figure xml:id="cktloopmesh">
  					<caption>A circuit diagram consisting of four nodes, six branches, seven loops, and three meshes.</caption>
  					<image width="40%" xml:id="ch2-cktloopmesh">
    					<shortdescription>A circuit has a DC voltage source, DC current source, AC source, inductor, resistor, and capacitor. The circuit has four nodes. The inductor, AC source, and capacitor connect at node a. The capacitor, high potential end of the DC voltage source, and resistor connect at node b. The resistor, AC source, and DC current source connect at node c. The DC current source flows out of node c. The inductor, low potential end of the DC voltage source, and the DC current source connect at node d.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image >
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\draw (0,2) to [V, -*,] (0,0);
								\draw (0,2) to [R, *-*] (2,2);
								\draw (-2,3.5) to [short] (-2,2);
								\draw (2,3.5) to [short] (2,2);
								\draw (0,2) to [C, *-*] (-2,2);
								\draw (-2,2) to [L] (-2,0);
								\draw (2,2) to [I] (2,0);
								\draw (-2,3.5) to [sV] (2,3.5);
								\draw (-2,0) to [short] (2,0);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>The three meshes of the circuit in <xref ref="cktloopmesh" /> consist of loops that do not have any other loops inside of them. These are shown in red in <xref ref="circuitMeshes" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="circuitMeshes">
  					<caption>The three meshes (each shown in red) from the circuit in <xref ref="cktloopmesh" />.</caption>
  					<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-circuitMeshes">
    					<shortdescription>The circuit described above is drawn three times. Left: the mesh containing the inductor, capacitor, and DC voltage source is drawn in red. Middle: the mesh containing the DC voltage source, resistor, and DC current source is drawn in red. Right: the mesh containing the capacitor, resistor, and AC source is drawn in red.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image >
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\foreach \x/\thickA/\thickB/\thickC/\thickAB/\thickBC/\thickAC in {0/red/black/black/red/black/red, 5.5/black/red/black/red/red/black, 11/black/black/red/black/red/red} {
									\draw[\thickAB]		(\x,2) to [V, -*, color=\thickAB]		(\x,0);
									\draw[\thickBC]		(\x,2) to [R, *-*, color=\thickBC]		(\x+2,2);
									\draw[\thickC]			(\x-2,3.5) to [short, color=\thickC]	(\x-2,2);
									\draw[\thickC]			(\x+2,3.5) to [short, color=\thickC]	(\x+2,2);
									\draw[\thickAC]			(\x,2) to [C, *-*, color=\thickAC]		(\x-2,2);
									\draw[\thickA]			(\x-2,2) to [L, color=\thickA]		(\x-2,0);
									\draw[\thickB]			(\x+2,2) to [I, color=\thickB]		(\x+2,0);
									\draw[\thickC]			(\x-2,3.5) to [sV, color=\thickC]	(\x+2,3.5);
									\draw[\thickB]			(\x,0) to [short, color=\thickB]		(\x+2,0);
									\draw[\thickA]			(\x-2,0) to [short, color=\thickA]	(\x,0);
								}
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

				<p>The seven loops in the circuit include the three meshes shown in <xref ref="circuitMeshes" /> as well as four additional loops, each of which contain other loops inside of them. These four additional loops are shown in <xref ref="circuitLoops" />.</p>

				<figure xml:id="circuitLoops">
  					<caption>The four additional circuit loops (each shown in red) from the circuit in <xref ref="cktloopmesh" />.</caption>
  					<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-circuitLoops">
    					<shortdescription>The circuit described above is drawn four times. Top left: the loop containing the inductor, DC current source, and inductor is drawn in red. Top right: the loop containing the DC current source, inductor, capacitor, and resistor is drawn in red. Bottom left: the loop containing the inductor, AC source, resistor, and DC voltage source is drawn in red. Bottom right: the loop containing the AC source, DC current source, DC voltage source, and capacitor is drawn in red.</shortdescription>
						<latex-image >
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
								\foreach \x/\y/\thickA/\thickB/\thickC/\thickD/\thickCD/\thickBC/\thickACD/\thickBD/\thickABC/\thickABD in {0/0/red/black/black/black/black/black/red/black/red/red, 5.5/0/black/red/black/black/black/red/black/red/red/red, 0/-5/black/black/red/black/red/red/red/black/red/black, 5.5/-5/black/black/black/red/red/black/red/red/black/red} {
									\draw[\thickCD]		(\x,\y+2) to [V, -*, color=\thickCD]		(\x,\y);
									\draw[\thickBC]		(\x,\y+2) to [R, *-*, color=\thickBC]		(\x+2,\y+2);
									\draw[\thickACD]	(\x-2,\y+3.5) to [short, color=\thickACD]	(\x-2,\y+2);
									\draw[\thickACD]	(\x+2,\y+3.5) to [short, color=\thickACD]	(\x+2,\y+2);
									\draw[\thickBD]		(\x,\y+2) to [C, *-*, color=\thickBD]		(\x-2,\y+2);
									\draw[\thickABC]	(\x-2,\y+2) to [L, color=\thickABC]		(\x-2,\y);
									\draw[\thickABD]	(\x+2,\y+2) to [I, color=\thickABD]		(\x+2,\y);
									\draw[\thickACD]	(\x-2,\y+3.5) to [sV, color=\thickACD]		(\x+2,\y+3.5);
									\draw[\thickABC]	(\x-2,\y) to [short, color=\thickABC]		(\x,\y);
									\draw[\thickABD]	(\x,\y) to [short, color=\thickABD]		(\x+2,\y);
								}
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
  					</image>
				</figure>

			</statement>
		</example>

	</section>


    <section xml:id="ch2-resistor">
    	<title>Resistors</title>
    
		<introduction>

			<p>A <term>resistor</term> is a circuit element that regulates the flow of current. This is the first passive element that will be studied in detail in this textbook. <term>Resistance</term>, which is a property of resistors, defines how much the resistor opposes the flow of current. Resistance has a symbol of <m>R</m> and a unit of &#8486; (ohm).</p>

			<remark>
				<title>Summary of resistance</title>

				<p>
					<ul>
						<li><p>Symbol: <m>R</m></p></li>
						<li><p>Unit: &#8486; (ohm)</p></li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</remark>

			<p>For an object with a uniform cross section, resistance is defined by <xref ref="ch2-resistoreq" />, where <m>\rho</m> is the <term>resistivity</term> of the object (this is property inherent to a material), <m>l</m> is the length of the object, and <m>A</m> is the cross sectional area of the object.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch2-resistoreq">
					R = \frac{\rho l}{A}
				</md>
			</p>

			<remark>
				<title>Summary of resistivity</title>

				<p>
					<ul>
						<li><p>Symbol: <m>\rho</m></p></li>
						<li><p>Unit: &#8486;m (ohm-meter)</p></li>
					</ul>
				</p>
			</remark>

			<p>The symbol used for a resistor in circuit diagrams is shown in <xref ref="resistorsymbol" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="resistorsymbol">
  				<caption>The circuit symbol for a resistor.</caption>
  				<image width="30%" xml:id="ch2-resistorsymbol">
    				<shortdescription>A zig-zag shape.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw (0,2) to [R] (3,2);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Resistors have very many important applications in circuits (tuning resonant frequencies, creating particular amplifier gains, or to create particular voltage or current values, among other things), and are therefore an important circuit element to understand.</p>

			<p>Physically, resistors come in many shapes, sizes, and forms. There are fixed resistors (resistors whose resistance value is not meant to change) and variable resistors (which will be discussed in more detail in <xref ref="ch2-varresistors" />). Most typical hobbyist resistors are made from carbon film deposited on a piece of ceramic, with the ends of the carbon film connected to metal wires (known as leads). A helix shape is carved into the carbon film; it is the pitch of the helix and thickness of the carbon film that determines the resistance of the device. Carbon film resistors typically have tolerances between 2%<mdash />5%.</p>

		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch2-resistorcolorcodes">
			<title>Resistor Color Codes</title>

			<p>Carbon film resistors are coated and painted with colored stripes to distinguish each resistor's value in ohms. Most of these resistors contain four stripes (although five- and six-stripe versions do exist, they will not be discussed in this book). The first and second bands dictate the value of the resistance. The third band acts as a multiplier, and the tolerance band denotes how accurate the value of the resistor is. These stripes are depicted in <xref ref="resistorcolorbands" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="resistorcolorbands">
  				<caption>Depiction of each of the colored bands on a four-band carbon film resistor. This particular resistor has brown-black-red-gold stripes, and therefore has a value of <m>R = 1000~\Omega \pm 5\%</m>.</caption>
  				<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-resistorcolorbands">
    				<shortdescription>Drawing of a resistor, which appears as a rectangle with silver lines protruding from both short ends. The rectangle is white with four stripes. The left stripe is brown and labeled "first band (brown)." The next stripe is black and labeled "second band (black)." The next stripe is red and labeled "multiplier (red)." The right stripe is gold and labeled "tolerance (gold)."</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[ultra thick, gray] (-1.5,0.5) --++ (right:6);
							\fill[white] (0,0) rectangle (3,1);
							\foreach \col/\x/\y/\label in {brown/0.25/1/first band (brown), black/0.75/1.5/second band (black), red/1.25/2/multiplier (red), brown!30!yellow/1.75/2.5/tolerance (gold)} {
								\fill[\col]		(\x,0) rectangle ++(0.25,1);
								\draw[-Stealth]	(\x+0.125,0) --++ (down:\y) --++ (left:0.5) --++ (left:\x) node[left] {\label};
							}
							\draw[] (0,0) rectangle (3,1);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Each of the stripe colors depicts a particular value, outlined in <xref ref="tabresistorcolorvalues" />.</p>

			<table xml:id="tabresistorcolorvalues">
				<title>Descriptions of each of the colors used to denote values on a resistor</title>
  				<tabular halign="left" width="100%">
					<col width="25%" />
					<col width="25%" />
					<col width="25%" />
					<col width="25%" />
    				<row header="yes" bottom="medium">
      					<cell><p>Color</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>Value (1st and 2nd Bands)</p></cell>
      					<cell><p>Multiplier (3rd Band)</p></cell>
						<cell><p>Tolerance (4th Band)</p></cell>
	    			</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>Black</p></cell>
      					<cell>0</cell>
      					<cell><m>10^{0}~\Omega</m></cell>
      					<cell></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>Brown</p></cell>
      					<cell>1</cell>
      					<cell><m>10^{1}~\Omega</m></cell>
      					<cell><m>\pm1\%</m></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>Red</p></cell>
      					<cell>2</cell>
      					<cell><m>10^{2}~\Omega</m></cell>
      					<cell><m>\pm21\%</m></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>Orange</p></cell>
      					<cell>3</cell>
      					<cell><m>10^{3}~\Omega</m></cell>
      					<cell><m>\pm0.05\%</m></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>Yellow</p></cell>
      					<cell>4</cell>
      					<cell><m>10^{4}~\Omega</m></cell>
      					<cell><m>\pm0.02\%</m></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>Green</p></cell>
      					<cell>5</cell>
      					<cell><m>10^{5}~\Omega</m></cell>
      					<cell><m>\pm0.5\%</m></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>Blue</p></cell>
      					<cell>6</cell>
      					<cell><m>10^{6}~\Omega</m></cell>
      					<cell><m>\pm0.25\%</m></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>Violet</p></cell>
      					<cell>7</cell>
      					<cell><m>10^{7}~\Omega</m></cell>
      					<cell><m>\pm0.1\%</m></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>Gray</p></cell>
      					<cell>8</cell>
      					<cell></cell>
      					<cell><m>\pm0.01\%</m></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>White</p></cell>
      					<cell>9</cell>
      					<cell></cell>
      					<cell></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>Gold</p></cell>
      					<cell></cell>
      					<cell><m>10^{-1}~\Omega</m></cell>
      					<cell><m>\pm5\%</m></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>Silver</p></cell>
      					<cell></cell>
      					<cell><m>10^{-2}~\Omega</m></cell>
      					<cell><m>\pm10\%</m></cell>
					</row>
    				<row bottom="minor">
      					<cell><p>no band</p></cell>
      					<cell></cell>
      					<cell></cell>
      					<cell><m>\pm20\%</m></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</table>

			<p>To determine the value of a resistor, it is important to first establish which band corresponds to the first, and which corresponds to the tolerance band. Usually, the first band is painted closer to the lead than the tolerance band is. Otherwise, it can be helpful to note that the large majority of inexpensive hobbyist carbon-film resistors have a gold tolerance band, which also can help orient the resistor (but this trick will not be valid with any other tolerance value!). The first and second bands give the value of the resistor. Then, the multiplier is used to determine the weight of the value. The tolerance band depicts the range of percentages over which the actual value of the fabricated resistor is valid.</p>

			<example>
				<title>Calculating the resistance value using the color code</title>

				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the resistance value of a resistor with stripes colored yellow, violet, yellow, and gold.</p>

					<p>The first two stripes denote the value of the resistor: 47.</p>

					<p>The third stripe denotes the multiplier: <m>10^4</m>. This means the resistor has a value of 470,000<nbsp />&#8486; or 470<nbsp />k&#8486;.</p>

					<p>The fourth band denotes the tolerance: <m>\pm5\%</m>. This means the actual value of the resistor may be anywhere between 446.5<nbsp />k&#8486; and 493.5<nbsp />k&#8486;.</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch2-varresistors">
			<title>Variable Resistors and Potentiometers</title>

			<p>A <term>variable resistor</term> is a resistor whose value is not fixed but that can be altered by the user. Variable resistors used to be called <term>rheostats</term>, but that term is falling out of favor. The circuit symbol used for variable resistors is shown in <xref ref="varresistor" />. Note that a variable resistor is a two-terminal device.</p>

			<figure xml:id="varresistor">
  				<caption>The circuit symbol for a variable resistor.</caption>
  				<image width="30%" xml:id="ch2-varresistor">
    				<shortdescription>A zig-zag shape with an arrow pointed diagonally through the zig-zag.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw (0,2) to [vR] (3,2);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>A <term>potentiometer</term> is a three-terminal device that can be used as either a voltage divider or a variable resistor. The circuit symbol for a potentiometer is shown in <xref ref="potentiometer" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="potentiometer">
  				<caption>The circuit symbol for a potentiometer.</caption>
  				<image width="30%" xml:id="ch2-potentiometer">
    				<shortdescription>A zig-zag shape with an arrow pointed to the center of the zig-zag.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw (0,2) to [pR] (3,2);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Two fixed leads are connected to a resistive element (commonly made out of either carbon or wound wire) in a semi-circular configuration. A moveable lead called the <term>wiper</term> can then be positioned at any point along the resistive element. This is depicted in <xref ref="potcartoon" />. While the total resistance between the fixed leads is constant, the resistance between the wiper and either of the two fixed leads changes as the wiper's position changes.</p>

			<figure xml:id="potcartoon">
  				<caption>A diagram of a potentiometer. The gray semicircular area corresponds to the resistive element. Two leads (one at each end of the resistive element) are fixed. The third lead is a moveable wiper.</caption>
  				<image width="30%" xml:id="ch2-potcartoon">
    				<shortdescription>A potentiometer is depicted as a gray semicircular line. Each end of the semicircle is labeled "fixed end." An arrow points to a random point in the gray semicircle and is labeled "moveable wiper."</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[line width=7mm, gray!50] (1.5,0) arc (-45:225:1.5);
							\draw[*-, very thick] (-0.75,0.25) --++ (down:1) node[below, align=center] {fixed\\lead};
							\draw[*-, very thick] (1.65,0.25) --++ (down:1) node[below, align=center] {fixed\\lead};
							\draw[Stealth-, very thick] (-0.5,2.5) -- (0.45,1) --++ (down:3) node[below, align=center] {moveable\\wiper};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>A potentiometer can be used as a voltage divider. Voltage dividers (which will be explained in greater detail in <xref ref="ch2-voltagedivider" />) take a fixed voltage and proportionally divide it up into two or more values. If a fixed voltage is applied to the two fixed leads of a potentiometer, then the voltage drop between either lead and the wiper will be modified depending on the position of the wiper. This is depicted schematically in <xref ref="potvoltagedivider" />. Note that while <m>V_1</m> and <m>V_2</m> can change depending on the position of the wiper, <m>V_1 + V_2 = V_S</m> will always be satisfied.</p>

			<figure xml:id="potvoltagedivider">
  				<caption>A potentiometer can be used as a voltage divider. The relationship <m>V_1 + V_2 = V_S</m> is always preserved, regardless of the individual values of <m>V_1</m> and <m>V_2</m>.</caption>
  				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-potvoltagedivider">
    				<shortdescription>A circuit containing a DC voltage source and a potentiometer. The voltage V1 is measured between the high potential of the voltage source and the wiper. The voltage V2 is measured between the wiper and the low potential of the voltage source. Left: The wiper of the potentiometer is depicted as being in the center of the potentiometer. Middle: The wiper of the potentiometer is depicted as being above center. Right: The wiper of the potentiometer is depicted as being below center.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\foreach \x/\a in {0/2, 5/2.5, 10/1.5} {
								\draw[]		(\x,4) to [V, l_=$V_s$]	(\x,0);
								\draw[]		(\x,4) to [short]			(\x+2,4);
								\draw[]		(\x+2,4) to [R, resistors/width=1.5, resistors/zigs=6]	(\x+2,0);
								\draw[]		(\x,0) to [short]			(\x+2,0);
								\draw[Stealth-]	(\x+2.25,\a) --++ (right:0.75);
								\draw[]		(\x+2.75,4) to [open, v_=$V_1$]	(\x+2.75,\a);
								\draw[]		(\x+2.75,\a) to [open, v_=$V_2$]	(\x+2.75,0);
							}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>A potentiometer can also be used as a variable resistor. In this case, one of the fixed leads will be ignored, and the resistance between the other fixed lead and the wiper will be used. Because the wiper itself has a small amount of resistance, it should be shorted to the unused fixed lead. This is depicted schematically in <xref ref="potasvr" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="potasvr">
  				<caption>A potentiometer can be used as a variable resistor by configuring it as shown in the right-hand diagram. Both of these schematics are functionally equivalent.</caption>
  				<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-potasvr">
    				<shortdescription>Left: a circuit contains a DC voltage source wired in series with a variable resistor. Right: a circuit contains a DC voltage source wired in series with the two fixed leads of a potentiometer. The wiper is connected with a wire to the low potential end of the DC voltage source.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw (0,3) to [V, l_=$V_s$] (0,0);
							\draw (0,3) to [short] (3,3);
							\draw (3,3) to [vR] (3,0);
							\draw (0,0) to [short] (3,0);
							\draw (5,3) to [V, l_=$V_s$] (5,0);
							\draw (5,3) to [short] (8,3);
							\draw (8,3) to [pR, name=pot] (8,0);
							\draw (5,0) to [short] (8,0);
							\draw (pot.wiper) --++ (right:0.5) --++ (down:1.5);
							\draw (8,0) to [short, *-] (9.06,0);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch2-measuringresistance">
			<title>Measuring Resistance</title>

			<p>Resistance can be measured in a circuit using a device called an <term>ohmmeter</term>. The circuit symbol for an ohmmeter is shown in <xref ref="ohmmeter" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="ohmmeter">
  				<caption>Circuit symbol used for a ohmmeter.</caption>
  				<image width="15%" xml:id="ch2-ohmmeter">
    				<shortdescription>Circle with the capital Greek letter omega inside the circle.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image >
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw (0,0) to [rmeter, t=$\Omega$] (2,0);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>An ohmmeter contains two leads. When the leads are placed on either end of the elements of a circuit, a constant current is passed through the circuit elements and the resulting voltage is measured with an analog to digital converter. Using Ohm's law, the resistance of the circuit elements can be calculated and displayed.</p>

			<p>Because resistors don't have any polarity or direction to them, the ordering of the leads is unimportant (the common lead can be placed on either end of the circuit elements without impacting the result). However, because an ohmmeter operates by sending a current through the circuit element(s) and measuring the resulting voltage, it is important to know that <alert>resistance cannot be measured in a circuit unless all other power sources have been disconnected</alert>.</p>

			<p>Additionally, when measuring resistance in a circuit, it's important to disconnect any circuit elements that should not be included in the resistance calculation. This includes all other circuit elements, including sources (even if they are turned off). For example, in <xref ref="resistors-connected-for-ohmmeter" /> (left), the ohmmeter is measuring the resistance contributed by all four resistors. If, say, only the contribution from resistors <m>R_3</m> and <m>R_4</m> is desired, then the circuit should be wired as shown in <xref ref="resistors-connected-for-ohmmeter" /> (right), where <m>R_1</m> and <m>R_2</m> are removed from the circuit.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="resistors-connected-for-ohmmeter">
  				<caption>The ohmmeter on the left will calculate the resistance contribution from all four resistors. The ohmmeter on the right will calculate the resistance contribution from only resistors <m>R_3</m> and <m>R_4</m>.</caption>
				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch1-resistors-connected-for-ohmmeter">
					<shortdescription>Left: Circuit with three meshes and 3 nodes consisting of 4 resistors and an ohmmeter. R1 and R2 are connected at node a. R2, R3, R4, and the ohmmeter are connected at node b. R1, R3, R4, and the ohmmeter are connected at node c. Right: circuit with two meshes and 2 nodes consisting of resistor R3, resistor R4, and an ohmmeter. The three circuit elements are connected in parallel.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$]			(0,0);
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]			(2.5,2.5) --++ (right:5);
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_3$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
							\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_4$, *-*]		(5,0);
							\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [rmeter, t=$\Omega$]	(7.5,0) --++ (left:7.5);
							\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [short]					(15,2.5);
							\draw[]	(10,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_3$]			(10,0);
							\draw[]	(12.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_4$, *-*]	(12.5,0);
							\draw[]	(15,2.5) to [rmeter, t=$\Omega$]	(15,0) --++ (left:5);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Because an ohmmeter sends a constant current through a circuit and measures the resulting voltage, an ohmmeter must be connected in parallel across the components to be measured, as shown in <xref ref="measuringvoltage" />.</p>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch2-resistorsseries">
			<title>Resistors in Series</title>

			<p>The first tool to analyze and simplify resistors is to understand how to combine resistors in series. That is: all of the resistors are in the same path in a circuit and share the same current. A circuit diagram containing five resistors in series is shown in <xref ref="figresistorseries" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="figresistorseries">
  				<caption>Five resistors in series in a circuit.</caption>
  				<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-figresistorseries">
					<!-- if this yells at me, put shortdescription back in but blank -->
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\foreach \x in {0, 2, 4, 6, 8} {
								\draw (\x,0) to [R] (\x+2,0);
							}
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Without having formalized the concepts of Kirchhoff's laws yet (which will occur in <xref ref="ch2-kirchhoffs" />), we can use <xref ref="ch2-resistoreq" /> to form a conceptual understanding of what happens when resistors are connected together in series. Of the parameters given in <xref ref="ch2-resistoreq" />, the only one that effectively changes when resistors are connected in series is the length (<m>l</m>). The resistivity, being a property inherent to the material used to create the resistor, does not change. The cross-sectional area also remains unchanged throughout. This is depicted in <xref ref="resistorserieslength" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="resistorserieslength">
  				<caption>When resistors are connected in series, the parameter of <xref ref="ch2-resistoreq" /> that changes is the length.</caption>
  				<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-resistorserieslength">
    				<shortdescription>Five resistors in series appear to be contained in a long cylinder, with the resistors running along the length of the cylinder. The length of the cylinder is labeled "l1 + l2 + l3 + l4 + l5." The cross-sectional area of the cylinder is labeled "A."</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\foreach \x in {0, 2, 4, 6, 8} {
								\draw (\x,0) to [R] (\x+2,0);
							}
							\draw[fill=gray!50] (10,0) ellipse (0.3cm and 0.5cm);
							\draw (10,0.5) --++ (left:10);
							\draw (10,-0.5) --++ (left:10);
							\draw (0,0.5) arc(90:270:0.3cm and 0.5cm);
							\draw[Stealth-Stealth] (0,1) -- (10,1);
							\node[fill=white, align=center] at (5,1) {$l_1+l_2+l_3+l_4+l_5$};
							\draw[Stealth-] (10,0) --++ (1,0.5) node[above right] {$A$};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>Because the total length of the circuit is equal to the sum of the individual lengths of each resistor, we can express the total resistance (known as the <term>equivalent resistance</term>) of the circuit likewise, shown in <xref ref="ch2-resistorseriesequation" /> and derived in <xref ref="seriesresistanceproof" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch2-resistorseriesequation">
					R_{EQ} = R_1 + R_2 + R_3 + ... + R_n
				</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="seriesresistanceproof">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>R_{EQ}	\amp = \frac{\rho(l_1+l_2+l_3+...+l_n)}{A}</mrow>
						<mrow>			\amp = \frac{\rho l_1}{A} + \frac{\rho l_2}{A} + \frac{\rho l_3}{A} + ... + \frac{\rho l_n}{A}</mrow>
						<mrow>			\amp = R_1 + R_2 + R_3 + ... + R_n</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>Qualitatively, the resistance in a series circuit increases. It is therefore impossible for the equivalent resistance of a series circuit to be less than the value of any individual resistor. (This last property acts as a useful <q>smell test</q> when working out complicated circuits problems.)</p>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch2-resistorsparallel">
			<title>Resistors in Parallel</title>

			<p>The second tool that can be used to simplify complicated resistive circuits is equivalent resistance for resistors in parallel. In this case, each resistor shares a common node on either end of the circuit element, but each contains its own unique branch through which current can flow. A circuit diagram containing five resistors in parallel is shown in <xref ref="resistorparallel" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="resistorparallel">
  				<caption>Five resistors in parallel in a circuit.</caption>
  				<image width="40%" xml:id="ch2-resistorparallel">
					<!-- if this yells at me, put shortdescription back in but blank -->
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\foreach \x in {0, 1, 2, 3, 4} {
								\draw[]	(\x,0) to [R]		++(up:2);	
							}
							\foreach \x in {0, 1, 2} {
								\draw[]	(\x,0) to [short, -*]	++(right:1);
								\draw[]	(\x,2) to [short, -*]	++(right:1);
							}
							\draw (3,0) to [short] (4,0);
							\draw (3,2) to [short] (4,2);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>In this case, the effective parameter that changes in <xref ref="ch2-resistoreq" /> is the cross-sectional area (<m>A</m>), shown in <xref ref="resistorparallelarea" />. The length and resistivity remain unchanged.</p>

			<figure xml:id="resistorparallelarea">
  				<caption>When resistors are connected in parallel, the parameter of <xref ref="ch2-resistoreq" /> that changes is the area.</caption>
  				<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-resistorparallelarea">
    				<shortdescription>Five resistors in parallel appear to be contained in their own cylinders, with each resistor running along the length of the cylinder. The length of each cylinder is labeled "l." The cross-sectional areas of the cylinders are labeled "A1 + A2 + A3 + A4 + A5."</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\node[align=center] at (2,3.5) (text) {$A_1+A_2+A_3+A_4+A_5$};
							\foreach \x in {0, 1, 2, 3, 4} {
								\draw[]	(\x,0) to [R]		++(up:2);	
								\draw[fill=gray!50] (\x,2.3) ellipse (0.5cm and 0.2cm);
								\draw[]	(\x+0.5,2.3) --++ (down:2.5);
								\draw[]	(\x-0.5,-0.2) arc(180:360:0.5cm and 0.2cm);
								\draw[Stealth-] (\x,2.3) -- (text);
							}
							\foreach \x in {0, 1, 2} {
								\draw[]	(\x,0) to [short, -*]	++(right:1);
								\draw[]	(\x,2) to [short, -*]	++(right:1);
							}
							\draw (3,0) to [short] (4,0);
							\draw (3,2) to [short] (4,2);
							\draw (-0.5,2.3) --++ (down:2.5);		
							\draw[Stealth-Stealth] (-1,2.3) --++ (down:2.5);
							\node[fill=white, align=center] at (-1,1.05) {$l$};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>It is now possible to express the equivalent resistance of a circuit with parallel resistors using the equation shown in <xref ref="ch2-resistorparallelequation" /> and derived in <xref ref="parallelresistanceproof" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch2-resistorparallelequation">
					\frac{1}{R_{EQ}} = \frac{1}{R_1} + \frac{1}{R_2} + \frac{1}{R_3} + ... + \frac{1}{R_n}
				</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="parallelresistanceproof">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>R_{EQ}			\amp = \frac{\rho l}{A_1+A_2+A_3+...+A_n}</mrow>
						<mrow>					\amp = \frac{1}{\frac{A_1}{\rho l}+\frac{A_2}{\rho l}+\frac{A_3}{\rho l}+...+\frac{A_n}{\rho l}}</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{1}{R_{EQ}}	\amp = \frac{A_1}{\rho l} + \frac{A_2}{\rho l} + \frac{A_3}{\rho l} + ... + \frac{A_n}{\rho l}</mrow>
						<mrow>					\amp = \frac{1}{R_1} + \frac{1}{R_2} + \frac{1}{R_3} + ... + \frac{1}{R_n}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

			<p>Qualitatively, the resistance in a parallel circuit decreases. The equivalent resistance cannot be greater than the value of any of the individual resistors.</p>

			<p>Frequently, it is necessary to combine two resistors in parallel. In that case, we can solve <xref ref="ch2-resistorparallelequation" /> to find a handy equation to use without having to calculate the reciprocal of the sum of reciprocals. (Note: this equation will <alert>only</alert> work for two resistors in parallel.) This solution is shown in <xref ref="ch2-eqtworesistorsparallel" /> and is derived in <xref ref="twoparallelresistorsproof" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="ch2-eqtworesistorsparallel">
					R_{EQ} = \frac{R_1R_2}{R_1+R_2}
				</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="twoparallelresistorsproof">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>\frac{1}{R_{EQ}}	\amp = \frac{1}{R_1} + \frac{1}{R_2}</mrow>
						<mrow>					\amp = \frac{R_2}{R_1R_2} + \frac{R_1}{R_1R_2}</mrow>
						<mrow>					\amp = \frac{R_1+R_2}{R_1R_2}</mrow>
						<mrow> R_{EQ}			\amp = \frac{R_1R_2}{R_1+R_2}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch2-equivalentresistance">
			<title>Equivalent Resistance</title>

			<p>It is important to point out that most circuits are not connected with all resistors connected 100% series or 100% parallel. Many circuits have segments that contain elements in parallel and segments that contain elements in series. In our discussion of delta-wye transforms in <xref ref="ch2-deltawye-wyedelta" />, we will see that there are some resistor combinations that are neither parallel nor series, but require a transformation before they can be made into parallel or series combinations.</p>

			<p>The equivalent resistance in a circuit refers to the amount of resistance <q>seen</q> between two particular nodes. (While we know that there are no eyeballs looking between nodes <mdash /> at least, there are none depicted on the circuit diagram <mdash /> this phrase is commonly used to describe the calculation of equivalent resistance between two nodes.)  The equivalent resistance <q>seen</q> between two nodes indicates the resistance that would be measured between the two nodes.</p>

			<p>Equivalent resistance can be calculated by combining resistors using the parallel and series combinations explained previously. Usually this is an iterative process. Start by combining any resistors that are <alert>purely</alert> in parallel with each other, or that are <alert>purely</alert> in series with each other. Then, reassess the circuit and see if any more opportunities for combinations arise. Continue until the circuit contains as few elements as possible.</p>

			<p>The symbology that will be used in equivalent resistance equations in this textbook is a <m>+</m> sign for series combination (as the resistances are indeed just being added together) and a <m>//</m> symbol for parallel combinations. The order of operations is to deal with everything inside of parenthesis first; after that calculate parallel combinations; finally, calculate series combinations.</p>

			<example>
				<title>Calculating equivalent resistance in a circuit</title>
				
				<statement>
					<p>Consider the circuit shown in <xref ref="eqresistanceckt" />. Note that the resistors are not connected purely in series nor are they connected purely in parallel. In addition, we cannot start combining together circuit elements until we know which nodes to use to calculate the equivalent resistance. It is possible to calculate the equivalent resistance between any two nodes in the circuit. Not all of the circuit nodes are shown; just a few have been selected to demonstrate different equivalent resistance calculations.</p>

					<figure xml:id="eqresistanceckt">
  						<caption>A circuit composed of nine resistors that are not connected totally in series or totally in parallel.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-eqresistanceckt">
    						<shortdescription>A circuit composed of three meshes and nine resistors. The left mesh contains resistors R1, R2, R3, R4, and R5. The center mesh contains resistors R4, R5, R6, and R7. The right mesh contains resistors R7, R8, and R9. Six nodes are labeled. Node a is between R1 and R3. Nodes b and f are equivalent and are at the junction of R2, R5, R7, and R9. Node c is between R4 and R5. Node d is between R6 and R7. Node e is between R8 and R9.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,4) node[above left] {a} to [R, l_=$R_1$, o-] (0,2);
									\draw (0,2) to [R, l_=$R_2$, -o] (0,0) node[below left] {b};
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l^=$R_3$] (2,4);
									\draw (2,4) to [R, *-, l_=$R_4$, *-o] (2,2) node[left] {c};
									\draw (2,2) to [R, -*, l_=$R_5$] (2,0);
									\draw (2,4) to [short] (4,4);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, *-, l_=$R_6$, *-o] (4,2) node[right] {d};
									\draw (4,2) to [R, -*, l_=$R_7$] (4,0);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, l^=$R_8$] (6,4);
									\draw (6,4) node[above right] {e} to [R, l_=$R_9$, o-o] (6,0) node[below right] {f};
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (6,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>While it might be tempting to immediately combine resistors <m>R_4</m> and <m>R_5</m> in series, resistors <m>R_6</m> and <m>R_7</m> in series, and then those two combinations in parallel with each other, it is possible to calculate an equivalent resistance between nodes c and d; in that case, those resistors cannot be combined! Therefore, it is important to pay attention to the nodes to be used to calculate the equivalent resistance before combining resistors together. We will use this circuit to calculate equivalent resistance between three different pairs of nodes to demonstrate the process. In all cases, it is wise to start combining resistors as far away from the reference nodes as possible.</p>
					
					<p><alert>Calculating the equivalent resistance between nodes a and b</alert></p>

					<p>Schematically, it's possible to depict equivalent resistance measured between two nodes by clearly indicating them on a circuit diagram. This is shown for the calculation of equivalent resistance between nodes a and b in <xref ref="eqresistancecktab" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="eqresistancecktab">
  						<caption>Schematic depiction of the equivalent resistance between nodes a and b in the circuit given in <xref ref="eqresistanceckt" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-eqresistancecktab">
    						<shortdescription>The same circuit as above, with wires protruding out from nodes a and b to highlight where equivalent resistance should be calculated from.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (-2,4) node[left] {a} to [short, o-] (0,4);
									\draw (-2,0) node[left] {b} to [short, o-] (0,0);
									\node[align=right, anchor=east] at (-2,2) {$R_{EQ} \rightarrow$};
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-] (0,2);
									\draw (0,2) to [R, l_=$R_2$, -*] (0,0);
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l^=$R_3$] (2,4);
									\draw (2,4) to [R, *-, l_=$R_4$] (2,2);
									\draw (2,2) to [R, -*, l_=$R_5$] (2,0);
									\draw (2,4) to [short] (4,4);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, *-, l_=$R_6$] (4,2);
									\draw (4,2) to [R, -*, l_=$R_7$] (4,0);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, l^=$R_8$] (6,4);
									\draw (6,4) to [R, l_=$R_9$] (6,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (6,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>In this case, the resistors on the right-hand side of the schematic are as far from the reference nodes as possible. First, combine resistors <m>R_8</m> and <m>R_9</m> in series, as they share a common path in the circuit. The circuit is re-drawn below.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="eqresistancecktab2">
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-eqresistancecktab2">
    						<shortdescription>The same circuit as above, with R8 and R9 combined in series.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (-2,4) node[left] {a} to [short, o-] (0,4);
									\draw (-2,0) node[left] {b} to [short, o-] (0,0);
									\node[align=right, anchor=east] at (-2,2) {$R_{EQ} \rightarrow$};
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-] (0,2);
									\draw (0,2) to [R, l_=$R_2$, -*] (0,0);
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l^=$R_3$] (2,4);
									\draw (2,4) to [R, *-, l_=$R_4$] (2,2);
									\draw (2,2) to [R, -*, l_=$R_5$] (2,0);
									\draw (2,4) to [short] (6,4);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, *-, l_=$R_6$] (4,2);
									\draw (4,2) to [R, -*, l_=$R_7$] (4,0);
									\draw (6,4) to [R, l_=$R_8+R_9$] (6,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (6,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
					
					<p>Resistors <m>R_4</m> and <m>R_5</m> can be combined in series, as can resistors <m>R_6</m> and <m>R_7</m>. This is shown below.</p>

					<figure xml:id="eqresistancecktab3">
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-eqresistancecktab3">
    						<shortdescription>The same circuit as above, with R4 and R5 combined in series and R6 and R7 combined in series.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (-2,4) node[left] {a} to [short, o-] (0,4);
									\draw (-2,0) node[left] {b} to [short, o-] (0,0);
									\node[align=right, anchor=east] at (-2,2) {$R_{EQ} \rightarrow$};
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-] (0,2);
									\draw (0,2) to [R, l_=$R_2$, -*] (0,0);
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l^=$R_3$] (2,4);
									\draw (2,4) to [R, *-*, l_=$R_4+R_5$] (2,0);
									\draw (2,4) to [short] (6,4);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, *-*, l_=$R_6+R_7$] (4,0);
									\draw (6,4) to [R, l_=$R_8+R_9$] (6,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (6,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
						
					<p>Those equivalent resistances of <m>R_4+R_5</m>, <m>R_6+R_7</m>, and <m>R_8+R_9</m> can now be combined in parallel. This is shown below.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="eqresistancecktab4">
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-eqresistancecktab4">
    						<shortdescription>The same circuit as above, with (R4+R5) in parallel with (R6+R7) and (R8+R9).</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (-2,4) node[left] {a} to [short, o-] (0,4);
									\draw (-2,0) node[left] {b} to [short, o-] (0,0);
									\node[align=right, anchor=east] at (-2,2) {$R_{EQ} \rightarrow$};
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-] (0,2);
									\draw (0,2) to [R, l_=$R_2$, -*] (0,0);
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l^=$R_3$] (2,4);
									\draw (2,4) to [R, l^=$(R_4+R_5)//(R_6+R_7)//(R_8+R_9)$] (2,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (2,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
					
					<p>That parallel resistor combination can now be added in series with <m>R_3</m>. This is shown below.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="eqresistancecktab5">
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-eqresistancecktab5">
    						<shortdescription>The same circuit as above, with the parallel combination combined in series with R3.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (-2,4) node[left] {a} to [short, o-] (2,4);
									\draw (-2,0) node[left] {b} to [short, o-] (0,0);
									\node[align=right, anchor=east] at (-2,2) {$R_{EQ} \rightarrow$};
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-] (0,2);
									\draw (0,2) to [R, l_=$R_2$, -*] (0,0);
									\draw (2,4) to [R, l^=$R_3+(R_4+R_5)//(R_6+R_7)//(R_8+R_9)$] (2,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (2,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Resistors <m>R_1</m> and <m>R_2</m> are connected in series and can be combined. This is shown below.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="eqresistancecktab6">
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-eqresistancecktab6">
    						<shortdescription>The same circuit as above, with R1 and R2 combined in series.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (-2,3) node[left] {a} to [short, o-] (2,3);
									\draw (-2,0) node[left] {b} to [short, o-] (0,0);
									\node[align=right, anchor=east] at (-2,1.5) {$R_{EQ} \rightarrow$};
									\draw (0,3) to [R, l_=$R_1+R_2$, *-*] (0,0);
									\draw (2,3) to [R, l^=$R_3+(R_4+R_5)//(R_6+R_7)//(R_8+R_9)$] (2,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (2,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Finally, both sets of equivalent resistances can be combined in parallel This is shown below.</p>

					<figure xml:id="eqresistancecktab7">
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-eqresistancecktab7">
    						<shortdescription>The same circuit as above, with R1 and R2 combined in series.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (-2,3) node[left] {a} to [short, o-] (0,3);
									\draw (-2,0) node[left] {b} to [short, o-] (0,0);
									\node[align=right, anchor=east] at (-2,1.5) {$R_{EQ} \rightarrow$};
									\draw (0,3) to [R, l^=$(R_1+R_2)//(R_3+(R_4+R_5)//(R_6+R_7)//(R_8+R_9))$] (0,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
						
					<p>The equivalent resistance as seen between nodes a and b is depicted mathematically in <xref ref="eqresistancecktabcalc" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="eqresistancecktabcalc">
							R_{EQ,ab} = ((R_8+R_9)//(R_6+R_7)//(R_4+R_5)+R_3)//(R_1+R_2)
						</md>
					</p>

					<p><alert>Calculating the equivalent resistance between nodes e and f</alert></p>

					<p>Next, let's consider the case of calculating the equivalent resistance between nodes e and f. This is depicted schematically in <xref ref="eqresistancecktef" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="eqresistancecktef">
  						<caption>Schematic depiction of the equivalent resistance between nodes e and f in the circuit given in <xref ref="eqresistanceckt" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-eqresistancecktef">
    						<shortdescription>The same circuit as above, with wires protruding out from nodes e and f to highlight where equivalent resistance should be calculated from.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\node[align=right, anchor=west] at (8,2) {$\leftarrow R_{EQ}$};
									\draw[]	(6,4) to [short, -o]			(8,4) node[right] {e} ;
									\draw[]	(6,0) to [short, -o]			(8,0) node[right] {f};
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l_=$R_1$]			(0,2);
									\draw (0,2) to [R, l_=$R_2$]			(0,0);
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l^=$R_3$]			(2,4);
									\draw (2,4) to [R, *-, l_=$R_4$]		(2,2);
									\draw (2,2) to [R, -*, l_=$R_5$]		(2,0);
									\draw (2,4) to [short]					(4,4);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, *-, l_=$R_6$]		(4,2);
									\draw (4,2) to [R, -*, l_=$R_7$]		(4,0);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, l^=$R_8$]			(6,4);
									\draw (6,4) to [R, l_=$R_9$, *-*]		(6,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short]					(6,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>In this case, the resistors on the left-hand side of the schematic are as far from the reference nodes as possible. Resistors <m>R_1</m>, <m>R_2</m>, and <m>R_3</m> can be combined in series. This is shown below.</p>
						
					<figure xml:id="eqresistancecktef2">
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-eqresistancecktef2">
    						<shortdescription>The same circuit as above, with R1, R3, and R3 connected in series.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\node[align=right, anchor=west] at (8,2) {$\leftarrow R_{EQ}$};
									\draw[]	(6,4) to [short, -o]			(8,4) node[right] {e} ;
									\draw[]	(6,0) to [short, -o]			(8,0) node[right] {f};
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l_=$R_1+R_2+R_3$]	(0,0);
									\draw (2,4) to [R, *-, l_=$R_4$]		(2,2);
									\draw (2,2) to [R, -*, l_=$R_5$]		(2,0);
									\draw (0,4) to [short]					(4,4);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, *-, l_=$R_6$]		(4,2);
									\draw (4,2) to [R, -*, l_=$R_7$]		(4,0);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, l^=$R_8$]			(6,4);
									\draw (6,4) to [R, l_=$R_9$, *-*]		(6,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short]					(6,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Resistors <m>R_4</m> and <m>R_5</m> are connected in series and can be combined. In addition, resistors <m>R_6</m> and <m>R_7</m> are in series and can be combined. This is shown below. Note that resistors <m>R_8</m> and <m>R_9</m> are <alert>not</alert> in series, as they do not share the same branch!</p>

					<figure xml:id="eqresistancecktef3">
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-eqresistancecktef3">
    						<shortdescription>The same circuit as above, R4+R5 connected in series and R6+R7 connected in series.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\node[align=right, anchor=west] at (8,2) {$\leftarrow R_{EQ}$};
									\draw[]	(6,4) to [short, -o]			(8,4) node[right] {e} ;
									\draw[]	(6,0) to [short, -o]			(8,0) node[right] {f};
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l_=$R_1+R_2+R_3$]	(0,0);
									\draw (2,4) to [R, *-*, l_=$R_4+R_5$]	(2,0);
									\draw (0,4) to [short]					(4,4);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, *-*, l_=$R_6+R_7$]	(4,0);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, l^=$R_8$]			(6,4);
									\draw (6,4) to [R, l_=$R_9$, *-*]		(6,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short]					(6,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistances of <m>R_1+R_2+R_3</m>, <m>R_4+R_5</m>, and <m>R_6+R_7</m> are connected in parallel and can be combined. This is shown below.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="eqresistancecktef4">
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-eqresistancecktef4">
    						<shortdescription>The same circuit as above, with (R1+R2+R3) connected in parallel with (R4+R5) and (R6+R7).</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\node[align=right, anchor=west] at (4,1.5) {$\leftarrow R_{EQ}$};
									\draw[]	(2,3) to [short, -o]			(4,3) node[right] {e} ;
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]			(4,0) node[right] {f};
									\draw (0,3) to [R, l_=$(R_1+R_2+R_3)//(R_4+R_5)//(R_6+R_7)$]	(0,0);
									\draw (0,3) to [R, l^=$R_8$]			(2,3);
									\draw (2,3) to [R, l_=$R_9$, *-*]		(2,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>That equivalent resistance is in series with <m>R_8</m>. The updated circuit is shown below.</p>
					
					<figure xml:id="eqresistancecktef5">
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-eqresistancecktef5">
    						<shortdescription>The same circuit as above, with the parallel resistors in series with R8.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\node[align=right, anchor=west] at (4,1.5) {$\leftarrow R_{EQ}$};
									\draw[]	(0,3) to [short, -o]			(4,3) node[right] {e} ;
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]			(4,0) node[right] {f};
									\draw (0,3) to [R, l_=$(R_1+R_2+R_3)//(R_4+R_5)//(R_6+R_7)+R_8$]	(0,0);
									\draw (2,3) to [R, l_=$R_9$, *-*]		(2,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>That equivalent resistance can then be combined in parallel <m>R_9</m>. This is shown below.</p>

					<figure xml:id="eqresistancecktef6">
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-eqresistancecktef6">
    						<shortdescription>The same circuit as above, with the parallel resistors in parallel with R9.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\node[align=right, anchor=west] at (2,1.5) {$\leftarrow R_{EQ}$};
									\draw[]	(0,3) to [short, -o]			(2,3) node[right] {e} ;
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, -o]			(2,0) node[right] {f};
									\draw (0,3) to [R, l_=$)(R_1+R_2+R_3)//(R_4+R_5)//(R_6+R_7)+R_8)//R_9$]	(0,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
						
					<p>The equivalent resistance between nodes e and f is depicted mathematically in <xref ref="eqresistancecktefcalc" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="eqresistancecktefcalc">
							R_{EQ,ef} = ((R_1+R_2+R_3)//(R_4+R_5)//(R_6+R_7)+R_8)//R_9
						</md>
					</p>

					<p><alert>Calculating the equivalent resistance between nodes a and e</alert></p>

					<p>Finally, we will consider the case of equivalent resistance between nodes a and e. This is depicted schematically in <xref ref="eqresistancecktae" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="eqresistancecktae">
  						<caption>Schematic depiction of the equivalent resistance between nodes a and e in the circuit given in <xref ref="eqresistanceckt" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-eqresistancecktae">
    						<shortdescription>The same circuit as above, with wires protruding out from nodes a and e to highlight where equivalent resistance should be calculated from.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\node[align=center, anchor=south] at (3,5) {$R_{EQ}$\\$\downarrow$};
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [short, -o]			(0,5) node[above] {a} ;
									\draw[]	(6,4) to [short, -o]			(6,5) node[above] {e};
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-]		(0,2);
									\draw (0,2) to [R, l_=$R_2$]			(0,0);
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l^=$R_3$]			(2,4);
									\draw (2,4) to [R, *-, l_=$R_4$]		(2,2);
									\draw (2,2) to [R, -*, l_=$R_5$]		(2,0);
									\draw (2,4) to [short]					(4,4);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, *-, l_=$R_6$]		(4,2);
									\draw (4,2) to [R, -*, l_=$R_7$]		(4,0);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, l^=$R_8$]			(6,4);
									\draw (6,4) to [R, l_=$R_9$, *-]		(6,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short]					(6,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>In this case, resistors <m>R_1</m> and <m>R_2</m> are in series with each other. This is also true for resistors <m>R_4</m> and <m>R_5</m>, and for resistors <m>R_6</m> and <m>R_7</m>. This is shown below.</p>

					<figure xml:id="eqresistancecktae2">
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-eqresistancecktae2">
    						<shortdescription>The same circuit as above, with R1 and R2 in series; R4 and R5 in series; and R6 and R7 in series.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\node[align=center, anchor=south] at (3,5) {$R_{EQ}$\\$\downarrow$};
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [short, -o]			(0,5) node[above] {a} ;
									\draw[]	(6,4) to [short, -o]			(6,5) node[above] {e};
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l_=$R_1+R_2$, *-]	(0,0);
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l^=$R_3$]			(2,4);
									\draw (2,4) to [R, *-*, l_=$R_4+R_5$]	(2,0);
									\draw (2,4) to [short]					(4,4);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, *-*, l_=$R_6+R_7$]	(4,0);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, l^=$R_8$]			(6,4);
									\draw (6,4) to [R, l_=$R_9$, *-]		(6,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short]					(6,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistances of <m>R_4+R_5</m> and <m>R_6+R_7</m> are in parallel with each other and can be combined. This is shown below.</p>
						
					<figure xml:id="eqresistancecktae3">
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-eqresistancecktae3">
    						<shortdescription>The same circuit as above, with (R4+R5) in parallel with (R6+R7).</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\node[align=center, anchor=south] at (3,5) {$R_{EQ}$\\$\downarrow$};
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [short, -o]			(0,5) node[above] {a} ;
									\draw[]	(6,4) to [short, -o]			(6,5) node[above] {e};
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l_=$R_1+R_2$, *-]					(0,0);
									\draw (0,4) to [R, l^=$R_3$]							(4,4);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, *-*, l_=$(R_4+R_5)//(R_6+R_7)$]		(4,0);
									\draw (4,4) to [R, l^=$R_8$]							(6,4);
									\draw (6,4) to [R, l_=$R_9$, *-]						(6,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(6,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>At this point, none of the remaining resistors can be combined in either series or in parallel! For now, we are unable to move forward with the resistance calculation between nodes a and e. Eventually, we will solve this problem with delta-wye and wye-delta transforms (explained in <xref ref="ch2-deltawye-wyedelta" />). It is crucial to understand that <alert>not all resistors are necessarily going to be connected in parallel or series combinations.</alert></p>

				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>
	</section>


	<section xml:id="ch2-deltawye-wyedelta">
    	<title>Delta-Wye and Wye-Delta Transforms</title>

		<introduction>
    
    		<p>As mentioned previously, there are situations when a resistive circuit cannot be reduced using parallel and series combinations. In those cases, it may be possible to transform the resistor configuration to a functionally identical equivalent. After completing a transformation, series and parallel combinations may be possible. The two transformations that will be discussed in this book are delta-wye and wye-delta transforms.</p>

			<p>A delta circuit arrangement is shown in <xref ref="deltacircuitdiagram" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="deltacircuitdiagram">
  				<caption>A delta circuit.</caption>
  				<image width="40%" xml:id="ch2-deltacircuitdiagram">
    				<shortdescription>Three resistors (R1, R2, and R3) arranged in series in a triangle. The node between R1 and R3 is a. The node between R1 and R2 is b. The node between R2 and R3 is c.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw (0,0) node[below left] {b} to [R, l^=$R_1$, *-*] (1.5,1.5);
							\draw (0,0) to  [R, l_=$R_2$, *-*] (3,0);
							\draw (1.5,1.5) node[above] {a} to [R, l^=$R_3$, *-*] (3,0) node[below right] {c};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>The resistance between any two nodes in a delta circuit are parallel and series combinations of those resistors, defined in <xref ref="deltaabeq" />, <xref ref="deltabceq" />, and <xref ref="deltaaceq" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="deltaabeq">R_{ab} \amp = R_1//(R_2 + R_3)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="deltabceq">R_{bc} \amp = R_2//(R_1 + R_3)</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="deltaaceq">R_{ac} \amp = R_3//(R_1 + R_2)</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>A wye circuit arrangement is shown in <xref ref="wyecircuitdiagram" />. Note that it has one more node than a delta circuit.</p>

			<figure xml:id="wyecircuitdiagram">
  				<caption>A wye circuit.</caption>
  				<image width="40%" xml:id="ch2-wyecircuitdiagram">
    				<shortdescription>Three resistors (Ra, Rb, and Rc) arranged in a Y shape. All three resistors connect at a common node (not labeled). The dangling end of Ra is labeled "a," the dangling end of Rb is labeled "b," and the dangling end of Rc is labeled "c."</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw (0,0) to [R=$R_a$, *-*] (0,1.75) node[above] {a};
							\draw (0,0) to [R, l_=$R_b$, *-*] (-1.25,-1.25) node[below left] {b};
							\draw (0,0) to [R=$R_c$, *-*] (1.25,-1.25) node[below right] {c};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>The resistance between any two nodes in a wye circuit are series combinations of those resistors, defined in <xref ref="wyeabeq" />, <xref ref="wyebceq" />, and <xref ref="wyeaceq" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="wyeabeq">R_{ab} \amp = R_a + R_b</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="wyebceq">R_{bc} \amp = R_b + R_c</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="wyeaceq">R_{ac} \amp = R_a + R_c</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch2-deltawye">
			<title>Delta-Wye Transforms</title>

			<p>A delta-wye transform modifies a delta arrangement of resistors (<xref ref="deltacircuitdiagram" />) and converts it into an equivalent wye arrangement (<xref ref="wyecircuitdiagram" />).</p>

			<p>The equivalent resistance between any two nodes of the wye circuit must be identical to the corresponding equivalent resistances of the delta circuit in order to make this transformation valid. <xref ref="deltaabeq" /> is set equal to <xref ref="wyeabeq" />; <xref ref="deltabceq" /> is set equal to <xref ref="wyebceq" />; and <xref ref="deltaaceq" /> is set equal to <xref ref="wyeaceq" />. Then, the terms <m>R_a</m>, <m>R_b</m>, and <m>R_c</m> are solved for in terms of the resistances <m>R_1</m>, <m>R_2</m>, and <m>R_3</m>. (The algebraic details of these derivations will not be shown in this book.)</p>

			<p>The results are described in <xref ref="deltawyea" />, <xref ref="deltawyeb" />, and <xref ref="deltawyec" />. These equations govern the delta-wye transform.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="deltawyea">R_a \amp = \frac{R_1R_3}{R_1+R_2+R_3}</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="deltawyeb">R_b \amp = \frac{R_1R_2}{R_1+R_2+R_3}</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="deltawyec">R_c \amp = \frac{R_2R_3}{R_1+R_2+R_3}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<example>
				<title>Using a delta-wye transform to calculate equivalent resistance</title>

				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit shown in <xref ref="eqresistanceckt" /> between nodes c and d, given that each of the resistors has a value of <m>R = 1~k\Omega</m>. The circuit has been re-drawn below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>The circuit depicted in <xref ref="eqresistanceckt" /> shown with 1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors and an ohmmeter between nodes c and d.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch2-deltawyeexample1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with nine 1k resistors and one ohmmeter. The circuit has four meshes. The left mesh contains five of the resistors. The middle top mesh contains two resistors and the ohmmeter. The middle bottom mesh contains two resistors and the ohmmeter. The right mesh contains four of the resistors.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\foreach \x/\y/\dir in {0/4/down, 0/2/down, 2/4/left, 2/4/down, 2/2/down, 4/4/down, 4/2/down, 6/4/left} {
										\draw[]	(\x,\y) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$] ++(\dir:2);
									}
									\draw[]	(2,2) to [rmeter, t=$\Omega$, *-*]	(4,2);
									\draw[]	(2,4) to [short, *-*]				(4,4);
									\draw[]	(6,4) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]		(6,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(2,0);
									\draw[]	(2,0) to [short, *-*]				(4,0);
									\draw[]	(4,0) to [short]					(6,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine all series resistors and re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch2-deltawyeexample2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with six resistors and one ohmmeter. The circuit has four meshes. The left mesh contains one 3k resistor and two 1k resistors. The middle top mesh contains two 1k resistors and the ohmmeter. The middle bottom mesh contains two 1k resistors and the ohmmeter. The right mesh contains two 1k resistors and one 2k resistor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\foreach \x/\y/\dir in {2/4/down, 2/2/down, 4/4/down, 4/2/down} {
										\draw[]	(\x,\y) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$] ++(\dir:2);
									}
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l_=3~k$\Omega$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [short]					(2,4);
									\draw[]	(2,4) to [short, *-*]				(4,4);
									\draw[]	(4,4) to [short]					(6,4);
									\draw[]	(2,2) to [rmeter, t=$\Omega$, *-*]	(4,2);
									\draw[]	(6,4) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$]		(6,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(2,0);
									\draw[]	(2,0) to [short, *-*]				(4,0);
									\draw[]	(4,0) to [short]					(6,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 2<nbsp />k&#8486; and 3<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors are connected in parallel and can be combined. This is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-deltawyeexample3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with five resistors and one ohmmeter. The circuit has three meshes. The left mesh contains one 1.2k resistor and two 1k resistors. The right-top mesh contains two 1k resistors and the ohmmeter. The right-bottom mesh contains two 1k resistors and the ohmmeter.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\foreach \x/\y/\dir in {2/4/down, 2/2/down, 4/4/down, 4/2/down} {
										\draw[]	(\x,\y) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$] ++(\dir:2);
									}
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l_=1.2~k$\Omega$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [short]					(2,4);
									\draw[]	(2,4) to [short, *-]				(4,4);
									\draw[]	(2,2) to [rmeter, t=$\Omega$, *-*]	(4,2);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(2,0);
									\draw[]	(2,0) to [short, *-]				(4,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>No more resistors that can be combined in either series or in parallel. A delta-wye transform can be performed to modify the circuit. To make it more obvious, the resistors have been slightly re-drawn below. The delta configuration is depicted in red with each node labeled.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-deltawyeexample4">
    						<shortdescription>Same circuit as previous. The left mesh has been re-drawn to look like a triangle shape with all three of its resistors shown in red.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\foreach \x/\y/\dir in {4/4/down, 4/2/down} {
										\draw[]	(\x,\y) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$] ++(\dir:2);
									}
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [short]					(4,4);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(4,0);
									\draw[]	(2,2) to [rmeter, t=$\Omega$, -*]	(4,2);
									\draw[red]	(0,4) node[above] {B} to [R, l_=1.2~k$\Omega$, color=red, *-*]		(0,0) node[below] {C};
									\draw[red]	(0,4) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$, color=red, -*]	(2,2) node[above] {A};
									\draw[red]	(0,0) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$, color=red]	(2,2);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate each of the wye resistor values using <xref ref="deltawyea" />, <xref ref="deltawyeb" />, and <xref ref="deltawyec" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_a \amp=	\frac{(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega+1.2~\textrm{k}\Omega+1~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= 312.5~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>R_b \amp=	\frac{(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)(1.2~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega+1.2~\textrm{k}\Omega+1~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= 375~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>R_c \amp=	\frac{(1.2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega+1.2~\textrm{k}\Omega+1~\textrm{k}\Omega} \amp= 375~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Re-draw the circuit with the resistors in their wye configuration.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch2-deltawyeexample5">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with six resistors and one ohmmeter. The circuit has two meshes. The top mesh contains a 312.5 ohm, 375 ohm, and 1000 ohm resistor and the ohmmeter. The bottom mesh contains a 312.5 ohm (shared with top), 375 ohm, and 1000 ohm resistor and the ohmmeter.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=1375~$\Omega$]			(4,4);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=1375~$\Omega$]			(4,0);
									\draw[]	(2,2) to [rmeter, t=$\Omega$, -*]		(4,2);
									\draw[]	(0,2) to [R, l^=312.5~$\Omega$, *-]		(2,2);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [short]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(4,4) to [short]						(4,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two 1375<nbsp />&#8486; resistors are in parallel with each other and can be combined. The updated circuit is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch2-deltawyeexample6">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two resistors and one ohmmeter. The two resistors are 687.5 ohms and 312.5 ohms and are in series with each other and with the ohmmeter.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=687.5~$\Omega$]		(4,4);
									\draw[]	(2,2) to [rmeter, t=$\Omega$]		(4,2);
									\draw[]	(0,2) to [R, l^=312.5~$\Omega$]		(2,2);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [short]					(0,2);
									\draw[]	(4,4) to [short]					(4,2);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two resistors are in series and can be combined.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="30%" xml:id="ch2-deltawyeexample7">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with one resistor and one ohmmeter. The circuit has one mesh. The mesh contains a 1000 ohm resistor and the ohmmeter.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=1000~$\Omega$]	(3,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [rmeter, t=$\Omega$]		(3,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]					(0,0);
									\draw[]	(3,2.5) to [short]					(3,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance between nodes c and d is therefore equal to 1000<nbsp />&#8486;.</p>

				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch2-wyedelta">
			<title>Wye-Delta Transforms</title>

			<p>A wye-delta transform modifies a wye arrangement of resistors (<xref ref="wyecircuitdiagram" />) and converts it into an equivalent delta arrangement (<xref ref="deltacircuitdiagram" />).</p>

			<p>To ensure the two circuits are equivalent, <xref ref="deltaabeq" /> is set equal to <xref ref="wyeabeq" />; <xref ref="deltabceq" /> is set equal to <xref ref="wyebceq" />; and <xref ref="deltaaceq" /> is set equal to <xref ref="wyeaceq" />. Then, the terms <m>R_1</m>, <m>R_2</m>, and <m>R_3</m> are solved for in terms of resistances <m>R_a</m>, <m>R_b</m>, and <m>R_c</m>. (The algebraic details of these derivations will not be shown in this book.)</p>

			<p>The results are given in <xref ref="wyedelta1eq" />, <xref ref="wyedelta2eq" />, and <xref ref="wyedelta3eq" />. These equations govern the wye-delta transform.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="wyedelta1eq">R_1 \amp= \frac{R_aR_b+R_bR_c+R_aR_c}{R_c}</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="wyedelta2eq">R_2 \amp= \frac{R_aR_b+R_bR_c+R_aR_c}{R_a}</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="wyedelta3eq">R_3 \amp= \frac{R_aR_b+R_bR_c+R_aR_c}{R_b}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<example>
				<title>Using a wye-delta transform to calculate equivalent resistance</title>

				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>The circuit to be reduced with a wye-delta transform.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-wyedeltaexample1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes. One mesh contains 3k (connected nodes a to b), 2k (nodes a to c), and 2k (connected b to c) resistors. The other mesh contains 2k (connected b to c), 4k (connected b to d), and 5k resistors (connected c to d). The desired equivalent resistance is between nodes a and d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, -o] ++(left:0.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=3~k$\Omega$, *-*] (2,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2,2.5) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$, -*] (4,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2,2.5) to [R, l_=4~k$\Omega$, -*] (2,0);
									\draw[]	(-0.5,0) to [short, o-] (4,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short] ++(up:1.5);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$] (4,4);
									\draw[]	(4,2.5) to [short] ++(up:1.5);
									\draw[]	(4,2.5) to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$] (4,0);
									\node[align=right, anchor=east] at (-0.5,1.25) {$R_{EQ}~\rightarrow$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>None of these resistors shares a branch, therefore none are in series. None of these resistors shares two nodes, therefore none are in parallel. A wye-delta transform is required. (Alternatively, a delta-wye transform could be performed. This will not be shown in this example.)</p>

					<p>The resistors in the wye-arrangement are shown in red. Each of the nodes is labeled.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>The circuit to be reduced with a wye-delta transform. The wye resistors are shown in red.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-wyedeltaexample2">
    						<shortdescription>Same circuit as before. The 3k (connected a to b), 2k (connected b to c), and 4k (connected b to d) resistors are shown in red.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\foreach \x/\y/\pos/\label in {0/2.5/above left/A, 4/2.5/right/B, 2/0/below/C, -0.5/1.25/left/$R_{EQ}~\rightarrow$} {
										\node[\pos] at (\x, \y) {\label};
									}
									\draw[]		(0,2.5) to [short, -o] 							++(left:0.5);
									\draw[]		(-0.5,0) to [short, o-]							(4,0);
									\draw[]		(0,2.5) to [short] 							++(up:1.5);
									\draw[]		(0,4) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$]					(4,4);
									\draw[]		(4,2.5) to [short]							++(up:1.5);
									\draw[]		(4,2.5) to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$]					(4,0);
									\draw[red]		(0,2.5) to [R, l^=3~k$\Omega$, *-*, color=red]		(2,2.5);
									\draw[red]		(2,2.5) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$, -*, color=red]		(4,2.5);
									\draw[red]		(2,2.5) to [R, l_=4~k$\Omega$, -*, color=red]		(2,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use <xref ref="wyedelta1eq" />, <xref ref="wyedelta2eq" />, and <xref ref="wyedelta3eq" /> to solve for <m>R_1</m>, <m>R_2</m>, and <m>R_3</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_1 \amp= \frac{26000~\Omega^2}{4000~\Omega} \amp= 6500~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>R_2 \amp= \frac{26000~\Omega^2}{3000~\Omega} \amp= 8670~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>R_3 \amp= \frac{26000~\Omega^2}{2000~\Omega} \amp= 13000~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-wyedeltaexample3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit now has three meshes and three nodes. One mesh has 2k and 6.5k (shared with next) resistors. Another mesh has 6.5k (shared with previous), 13k, and 8.67k (shared with next) resistors. The last mesh has 8.67k (shared with previous) and 5k resistors.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[above left] {A} to [short, -o]		++(left:0.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=6.5~k$\Omega$, *-*]			(4,2.5) node[right] {B};
									\draw[]	(4,2.5) to [R, l_=8.67~k$\Omega$, -*]			(2,0) node[below] {C};
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=13~k$\Omega$, -*]			(2,0);
									\draw[]	(-0.5,0) to [short, o-]						(4,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						++(up:1.5);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$]				(4,4);
									\draw[]	(4,2.5) to [short]						++(up:1.5);
									\draw[]	(4,2.5) to [R, l^=5~k$\Omega$]				(4,0);
									\node[align=right, anchor=east] at (-0.5,1.25) {$R_{EQ}~\rightarrow$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 2<nbsp />k&#8486; and 6.5<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors are in parallel and can be combined. Similarly, the 8.67<nbsp />k&#8486; and 5<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors are in parallel and can be combined. The updated circuit is shown below.</p>
					
					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-wyedeltaexample4">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with three resistors: 13k, 1.53k, and 3.17k. The 13k and 1.53k connect at node a. The 1.53k and 3.17k connect at node b. The 13k and 3.17k connect at node c. Equivalent resistance is measured between nodes a and c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, -o]						++(left:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=1.53~k$\Omega$]			(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=13~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short, o-]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=3.17~k$\Omega$]			(2.5,0);
									\node[align=right, anchor=east] at (-2.5,1.25) {$R_{EQ}~\rightarrow$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 1.53<nbsp />k&#8486; and 3.17<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors are in series and can be combined.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-wyedeltaexample5">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with three resistors: 13k, 1.53k, and 3.17k. The 13k and 1.53k connect at node a. The 1.53k and 3.17k connect at node b. The 13k and 3.17k connect at node c. Equivalent resistance is measured between nodes a and c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, -o]						++(left:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=13~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short, o-]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=4.7~k$\Omega$]			(2.5,0);
									\node[align=right, anchor=east] at (-2.5,1.25) {$R_{EQ}~\rightarrow$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two resistances are in parallel and can be combined. The final reduced circuit diagram is shown below.</p>
					
					<figure>
  						<caption></caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch2-wyedeltaexample6">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with one resistor of 3.45k.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, -o]						++(left:2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=3.45~k$\Omega$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short, o-]						(0,0);
									\node[align=right, anchor=east] at (-2.5,1.25) {$R_{EQ}~\rightarrow$};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>
	</section>

  	<section xml:id="ch2-ohmslaw">
  		<title>Ohm's Law</title>

		<introduction>

			<p>Ohm's law states the relationship between voltage, current, and resistance in a circuit. Current is proportional to voltage and inversely proportional to the resistance of the circuit. In equation form, Ohm's law is usually stated in one of three ways, shown in <xref ref="ohmslawveq" />, <xref ref="ohmslawieq" />, and <xref ref="ohmslawreq" />. Ohm's law is the first tool that we can use to analyze currents through and voltages over various elements in a circuit.</p> 

			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="ohmslawveq">V \amp= IR</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ohmslawieq">I \amp= \frac{V}{R}</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ohmslawreq">R \amp= \frac{V}{I}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>It is important to recall that voltage is a relative measurement (leading to a positive or negative result, depending on which way it is referenced) and that the sign of current relates to the direction. This means that it is important to pay attention to references and directions when using Ohm's law.</p>

			<p>It is vital to pay attention to the units used in Ohm's law. One ohm (the unit for resistance) is equal to one volt divided by one amp. If using a unit of ohm, all voltages must be expressed in volts and all currents must be expressed in amps. Frequently, resistance will be quantified with a unit of kilohm. If using a unit of kilohm, all voltages must be expressed in volts and all currents must be expressed in milliamps.</p>
			
			<p>When resistance is infinite in a branch, that branch is said to be <term>open</term>. Note that <xref ref="ohmslawieq" /> necessitates that, for sure, current will be equal to zero in an open branch. However, <xref ref="ohmslawveq" /> will be undefined, as the equation will be equal to zero times infinity. This means that voltage may not necessarily be equal to zero in an open. An open circuit is depicted in <xref ref="open-circuit-current-zero" />. The open branch depicted in <xref ref="open-circuit-current-zero" /> has a voltage <m>V</m> equal to that of the source, <m>V_S</m>.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="open-circuit-current-zero">
  				<caption>An open circuit (infinite resistance) with zero current through the branch. The voltage <m>V</m> will not be equal to zero in this circuit.</caption>
				<image width="30%" xml:id="ch2-open-circuit-current-zero">
					<shortdescription>Circuit with a voltage source Vs connected in series to a resistor R. There is no complete connection between the two circuit elements.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]	(0,0) --++ (right:2.5);
							\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R$, i^=$I{=}0$]		(2.5,2.5);
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [open, v_=$V$]		(2.5,0);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>When resistance is zero in a branch, that branch is said to be a <term>short</term>. Note that <xref ref="ohmslawveq" /> necessitates that, for sure, voltage will be equal to zero in an open branch. However, <xref ref="ohmslawieq" /> will be undefined, as the equation will be equal to zero divided by zero. This means that current may not necessarily be equal to zero through a short. A short is depicted in <xref ref="short-circuit-voltage-zero" />. The shorted branch depicted in <xref ref="short-circuit-voltage-zero" /> has a current <m>I</m> that can be calculated based on the values of the source and the two resistors.</p>
			
			<figure xml:id="short-circuit-voltage-zero">
  				<caption>A shorted circuit branch (zero resistance) with zero voltage drop over the short. The current <m>I</m> will not be equal to zero in this circuit.</caption>
				<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-short-circuit-voltage-zero">
					<shortdescription>Circuit with a current source Is connected in parallel to resistors R1 and R2. The branch between R1 and R2 is a short with voltage of 0 and undefined current.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]	(0,2.5) to [short] ++(right:2.5) to [short, v^=$V{=}0$, i_=$I$]	++(right:2.5);
							\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]	++(down:2.5);
							\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$]		++(down:2.5) --++ (left:5);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch2-calcvoltage">
			<title>Calculating Voltage from Current and Resistance</title>

			<p>If current and resistance are known, then the voltage drop can be calculated. Consider the resistor shown in <xref ref="referenceresistor" />. The value of the resistance has no polarity or direction. If the direction of current is from node a to node b, then node a corresponds to high potential and node b corresponds to low potential. In that case, <m>V_{ab}</m> (the electric potential at a minus the electric potential at b) is positive. If the direction of current is from node b to node a, then node b corresponds to high potential and <m>V_{ab}</m> would be negative.</p>

			<figure xml:id="referenceresistor">
  				<caption>A resistor used to demonstrate Ohm's law.</caption>
  				<image width="20%" xml:id="ch2-referenceresistor">
    				<shortdescription>A resistor labeled "R." The left lead of the resistor is labeled "a" and the right lead of the resistor is labeled "b."</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw (0,0) node[left] {a} to [R, l^=$R$, o-o] (2,0) node[right] {b};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>In other words, current flows from high potential to low potential in a resistor. The direction of the current can therefore be used to distinguish the high potential end of the circuit element (depicted with a <m>+</m> sign) from the low potential end (depicted with a <m>-</m> sign). This is depicted schematically in <xref ref="highlowpotential" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="highlowpotential">
  				<caption>Current flows from high to low potential in a resistor. Therefore, the direction of current can be used to determine which end of a circuit element corresponds to high potential and which corresponds to low potential.</caption>
  				<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-highlowpotential">
    				<shortdescription>Left: a resistor has a voltage drop from left to right, current flows from left to right. Right: a resistor has a voltage drop from right to left, current flows from right to left.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw (0,0) node[left] {a} to [R, f_=$I$, v^=$V$, o-o] (2,0) node[right] {b};
							\draw (6,0) node[right] {b} to [R, f^=$I$, v_=$V$, o-o] (4,0) node[left] {a};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<example>
				<title>Calculating voltage from current and resistance</title>

				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the voltage drop <m>V_X</m> across the resistors as shown in the circuit diagram below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>A circuit used to calculate an unknown voltage.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-voltagefromirex1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5mA source, 3k resistor, and 2k resistor. Circuit has two meshes and two nodes. The left mesh contains the 5 mA source and 3k resistor. Current flows "up" as defined by the diagram. The right mesh contains the 3k resistor and 2k resistor. The voltage over the 2k resistor is defined as Vx. This voltage drops from "bottom to top" as defined by the diagram.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,0) to [I, l^=5~mA] (0,2);
									\draw (0,2) to [short] (4,2);
									\draw (2,2) to [R, l_=3~k$\Omega$, *-*] (2,0);
									\draw (4,0) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$, v_=$V_X$] (4,2);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (4,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of the two resistors. The two resistors share both nodes, therefore they are in parallel.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{(3~\textrm{k}\Omega)(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega+3~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= 1.2~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit can be re-drawn.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>A simplified circuit used to calculate an unknown voltage.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch2-voltagefromirex2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5mA source and 1.2k resistor. Circuit has one mesh and two nodes. The mesh contains the 5 mA source and 1.2k resistor. Current flows "up" as defined by the diagram. The voltage over the 1.2k resistor is defined as Vx. This voltage drops from "bottom to top" as defined by the diagram.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=5~mA]					(0,2);
									\draw[]	(0,2) to [short]						(2,2);
									\draw[]	(2,0) to [R, l^=1.2~k$\Omega$, v_=$V_X$]	(2,2);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(2,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>V_X</m>. This voltage is defined in the opposite direction as the current, so its sign will be negative. (If the voltage had been defined in the other direction, then it would be measured in the same direction as the current, and the sign would be positive.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= (-5~\textrm{mA})(1.2~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= -6~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch2-calccurrent">
			<title>Calculating Current from Voltage and Resistance</title>

			<p>If the voltage drop over a circuit element as well as the resistance is known, then the current through that circuit element can be calculated. Current flows from high potential to low potential in a resistor. However, current can be assigned in any arbitrary direction; if the assignment of the current was from low to high potential, then a negative current will be calculated, as discussed in <xref ref="ch1-currentflow" />. Therefore, when calculating current in a circuit (as we will do in <xref ref="ch2-kcl" />), it is not important which direction is chosen to define current, as the real direction will make itself known with the sign of the result.</p>

			<p>This relationship between voltage drop and current direction assignment is depicted schematically in <xref ref="highlowcurrent" />.</p>

			<figure xml:id="highlowcurrent">
  				<caption>Current flows from high to low potential in a resistor. If the direction of current was chosen in this direction, current will be positive (left). If the flow was assigned in the opposite direction, current will be negative (right).</caption>
  				<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-highlowcurrent">
    				<shortdescription>Left: a resistor has a voltage drop from left to right, current is defined from left to right and is positive. Right: a resistor has a voltage drop from right to left, current is defined from left to right and is negative.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(0,0) node[left] {a} to [R, v^=$V$, o-o] (2.5,0) node[right] {b};
							\draw[-Stealth]	(1.5,-0.5) --++ (right:1) node[below left] {current is positive};
							\draw[]	(5,0) node[left] {a} to [R, v^=$V$, o-o] (7.5,0) node[right] {b};
							\draw[-Stealth]	(6,-0.5) --++ (left:1) node[below right] {current is negative};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<example>
				<title>Calculating current from voltage and resistance</title>

				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the current <m>I_X</m> through the resistors, as shown in the circuit diagram below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>A circuit used to calculate an unknown current.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch2-currentfromvrex1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh. The high potential end of an 8V source connects to one end of a 2k resistor. The other end of the 2k resistor connects to one end of a 3k resistor. The other end of the 3k resistor connects to the low potential end of the 8V source. Current Ix is defined as flowing toward the low potential end of the 8V source.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,2) to [V, l_=8~V] (0,0);
									\draw (0,2) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$] (2,2);
									\draw (2,2) to [R, l_=3~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_X$] (2,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (2,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance of the two resistors. The two resistors are both in the same branch and are therefore in series.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 2~\textrm{k}\Omega + 3~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= 5~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit can be re-drawn.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>A reduced circuit used to calculate an unknown current.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch2-currentfromvrex2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh. The high potential end of an 8V source connects to one end of a 5k resistor. The other end of the 5k resistor connects to the low potential end of the 8V source. Current Ix is defined as flowing toward the low potential end of the 8V source.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2) to [V, l_=8~V]					(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2) to [short]						(2,2);
									\draw[]	(2,2) to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_X$]	(2,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(2,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The current is flowing from high to low potential, so the sign (direction) of the current will be positive. (If the current had been defined in the other direction, then it would be measured from low to high potential and the sign would be negative.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_X \amp= \frac{8~\textrm{V}}{5~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= 1.6~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch2-calcresistance">
			<title>Calculating Resistance from Voltage and Current</title>

			<p>When using voltage and current to calculate resistance, it is important to ensure that the directionality of the current and the polarity of the voltage align with each other. In other words: if voltage is measured from high to low potential, then the current must be assigned to flow in that direction (from high to low). If the voltage was measured from low to high potential, then the current must be assigned to flow from low to high.</p>

			<p>In this textbook, the circuits that will be analyzed will all have a positive resistance. This is helpful in ensuring that our polarities/directions are assigned correctly, because we will find that we are always multiplying a positive voltage with a positive current or a negative voltage with a negative current, but never a positive with a negative. However, it is important to note that there are negative resistance circuits which may be encountered in a higher-level circuits class. In those cases, it will be crucial to pay attention to the assigned polarities and directions!</p>

			<example>
				<title>Calculating resistance from voltage and current</title>

				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the value of resistor <m>R_1</m> as shown in the circuit diagram below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>A circuit used to calculate an unknown resistance.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch2-resistancefromviex1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh. The high potential end of a 12V source connects to one end of R1. The other end of R1 connects to one end of a 100ohm resistor. The other end of the 100ohm resistor connects to the low potential end of the 12V source. A current of 30 mA is defined as flowing toward the low potential end of the 12V source.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,2) to [V, l_=12~V] (0,0);
									\draw (0,2) to [R, l^=R\textsubscript{1}] (2,2);
									\draw (2,2) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, i^=30~mA] (2,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (2,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use Ohm's law <xref ref="ohmslawreq" /> to solve this problem. The total resistance of this circuit is the series combination of <m>R_1</m> and the <m>100~\Omega</m> resistor.</p>

					<p>Voltage and current are both defined in compatible directions, so none of the signs need to be changed in the calculation. Because a unit of ohm is used in the calculation, the current through the circuit will be expressed with a unit of amp. (Alternatively, the resistance could be converted to k&#8486; while leaving the current in mA.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_1 + 100~\Omega \amp= \frac{12~\textrm{V}}{0.03~\textrm{A}}</mrow>
							<mrow>R_1 \amp= 400~\Omega - 100~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= 300~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch2-ohmpower">
			<title>Ohm's Law and the Power Equation</title>

			<p>Together with the power equation, it is possible to know any two of four properties (current, voltage, resistance, and power) and solve for the others. The equations that link Ohm's law and the power equation are shown in <xref ref="ohmspower1" />, <xref ref="ohmspower2" />, and <xref ref="ohmspower3" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="ohmspower1">P \amp= IV</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ohmspower2">P \amp= I^2R</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="ohmspower3">P \amp= \frac{V^2}{R}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>Just as we were concerned with units while utilizing Ohm's law, we are similarly interested in paying close attention to units when using the power equations. If ohms are used in <xref ref="ohmspower1" />, <xref ref="ohmspower2" />, and <xref ref="ohmspower3" />, then voltage must be expressed in volts, current must be expressed in amps, and the power unit will be expressed in watts. Alternatively, if kilohms are used for resistance, then voltage must be expressed in volts, current must be expressed in milliamps, and power will be expressed in milliwatts.</p>

			<example>
				<title>Power calculation</title>

				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the power consumed by the resistor in the circuit shown below. Then, calculate the value of the resistor.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>A circuit used to calculate power and the value of an unknown resistor.</caption>
  						<image width="30%" xml:id="ch2-powercalcex1">
    						<shortdescription>A resistor with high potential defined at the left and low potential defined at the right. The voltage drop in this direction is 25V. Current from left to right is defined as 10A.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,2) to [R, v^=25~V, i_=10~A] (3,2);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ohmspower1" /> to calculate the power consumed by the resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>P \amp= (10~\textrm{A})(25~\textrm{V})</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= 250~\textrm{W}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law <xref ref="ohmslawreq" /> to calculate the value of the resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R \amp= \frac{25~\textrm{V}}{10~\textrm{A}}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= 2.5~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</statement>
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>Calculating voltage</title>

				<statement>
					<p>In the circuit shown below, the 200<nbsp />&#8486; resistor is consuming 500<nbsp />mW of power. Calculate the value of the voltage source.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>A circuit used to calculate an unknown voltage.</caption>
  						<image width="30%" xml:id="ch2-voltagecalcex1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh. The high potential end of an voltage source Vs connects to one end of a 200ohm resistor. The other end of the 200ohm resistor connects to one end of a 400ohm resistor. The other end of the 400ohm resistor connects to the low potential end of the source.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,2) to [V, l_=$V_{S}$] (0,0);
									\draw (0,2) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$] (2,2);
									\draw (2,2) to [R, l_=400~$\Omega$] (2,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (2,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate the current that flows through the circuit by solving <xref ref="ohmspower2" /> for current. All units will be converted to V, A, &#8486;, and W.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I \amp= \sqrt{\frac{P}{R}}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= \sqrt{\frac{0.5~\textrm{W}}{200~\Omega}}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= 0.05~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law <xref ref="ohmslawveq" /> to calculate the value of the voltage source that is required to generate this current.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_S \amp= (0.05~\textrm{A})(200~\Omega + 400~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= 30~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

			<example>
				<title>Calculating minimum safe resistor value</title>

				<statement>
					<p>What is the minimum resistor value that can be used in the circuit shown below if the resistor is rated for a maximum power consumption of 1/4<nbsp />W?</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>A circuit used to calculate the minimum safe resistor value.</caption>
  						<image width="30%" xml:id="ch2-minimumsaferesistorex1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh containing a 10V source and resistor of unknown value.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,2) to [V, l_=10~V] (0,0);
									\draw (0,2) to [short] (2,2);
									\draw (2,2) to [R] (2,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (2,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ohmspower3" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{min} \amp= \frac{V^2}{P_{max}}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= \frac{(10~\textrm{V})^2}{0.25~\textrm{W}}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= 400~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>
				
	</section>

    <section xml:id="ch2-voltagedivider">
    	<title>Voltage Divider</title>

		<introduction>
    
  			<p>The voltage divider is a tool that uses Ohm's law to calculate the voltage dropped over resistive elements that are in series with each other without having to calculate current.</p>

			<p>In the simplest case, consider a circuit containing a voltage source (<m>V_S</m>) and two resistors (<m>R_1</m> and <m>R_2</m>), all wired in series. Ohm's law can be used to calculate the overall voltage/current relationship of the circuit, and is shown in <xref ref="voltagedivtotaleq" />. (We are calculating current here only to show how to derive the voltage divider rule.)</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="voltagedivtotaleq">I = \frac{V_S}{R_1+R_2}</md>
			</p>

			<p>Because everything is wired in series, this same current flows through both resistors. We can use Ohm's law to calculate the voltage drop over each resistor (<m>V_1=IR_1</m>, and <m>V_2=IR_2</m>), and plug in the result of <xref ref="voltagedivtotaleq" /> to remove the current term from the equation. The results are shown in <xref ref="voltagedivr1eq" /> and <xref ref="voltagedivr2eq" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes">
					<mrow xml:id="voltagedivr1eq">V_1 \amp= \frac{V_SR_1}{R_1+R_2}</mrow>
					<mrow xml:id="voltagedivr2eq">V_2 \amp= \frac{V_SR_2}{R_1+R_2}</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>

			<p>In general, <xref ref="voltagedividergeneraleq" /> can be used to calculate the voltage dropped over the <m>k^{\textrm{th}}</m> resistor of <m>n</m> resistors in series (where <m>k \lt n</m>), given a source voltage of <m>V_S</m>. As previously mentioned, there is no need to calculate current as long as the voltage and resistor values are known.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="voltagedividergeneraleq">V_k = V_S\left(\frac{R_k}{R_1+R_2+R_3+...+R_k+...+R_n}\right)</md>
			</p>

			<example>
				<title>Using the voltage divider rule</title>
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>V_{OUT}</m> in the circuit shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>A circuit used to demonstrate the voltage divider rule.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-voltagedividerexfig1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 20.5V source, 28k, 20k, 32k, 38k, 27k, and 30k resistors. There are three meshes and five nodes. High potential of 20.5V, 20k, and 28k connect at node a. 20k and 32k connect at node b. 38k, 28k, and 27k connect at node c. 27k and 30k connect at node d. Low potential of 20.5V, 38k, and 30k connect at node e. Vout is defined as high potential at c and low potential at e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,3) to [V, l_=20.5~V] (0,0);
									\draw (0,3) to [R, l^=20~k$\Omega$, *-] (2,3);
									\draw (2,3) to [R, l^=32~k$\Omega$, -*] (4,3);
									\draw (4,3) to [R, l_=38~k$\Omega$, -*] (4,0);
									\draw (4,3) to [short] (6,3);
									\draw (6,3) to [R, l_=27~k$\Omega$, *-] (6,1.5);
									\draw (6,1.5) to [R, l_=30~k$\Omega$] (6,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (6,0);
									\draw (0,3) to [short] (0,4.5);
									\draw (6,3) to [short] (6,4.5);
									\draw (0,4.5) to [R, l^=28~k$\Omega$] (6,4.5);
									\draw (6.75,3) to [open, v^=V\textsubscript{OUT}] (6.75,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine all resistors that are in series and re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>A circuit used to demonstrate the voltage divider rule, with all series resistors combined.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-voltagedividerexfig2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 20.5V source, 28k, 52k, 38k, and 57k resistors. Circuit has three meshes and three nodes. High potential of 20.5V, 28k, and 52k connect at node a. 52k, 28, 38, and 57k connect at node b. Low potential of 20.5V, 38k, and 57k connect at node c. Vout is defined as high potential at node b and low potential at node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20.5~V] (0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=52~k$\Omega$, *-] (2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=38~k$\Omega$, *-*] (2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short] (5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=57~k$\Omega$, *-, v^=V\textsubscript{OUT}] (5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] (5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short] (0,4);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short] (5,4);
									\draw[]	(0,4) to [R, l^=28~k$\Omega$] (5,4);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 28<nbsp />k&#8486; and 52<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors are in parallel with each other and have an equivalent resistance of 18.2<nbsp />k&#8486;. The 38<nbsp />k&#8486; and 57<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors are in parallel with each other and have an equivalent resistance of 22.8<nbsp />k&#8486;. The circuit can be re-drawn again.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>A circuit used to demonstrate the voltage divider rule, with all series and parallel resistors combined.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch2-voltagedividerexfig3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 20.5V, 18.2k, and 22.8k all in series. Vout is defined as dropping over 22.8k.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20.5~V] (0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=18.2~k$\Omega$] (2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=22.8~k$\Omega$, v^=V\textsubscript{OUT}] (2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] (2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate the output voltage.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp = 20.5~\textrm{V}\left(	\frac{22.8~\textrm{k}\Omega}{22.8~\textrm{k}\Omega + 18.2~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = 11.4~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

			<example xml:id="ch2-voltagedivider-twomesh-example">
				<title>Voltage divider with a two-mesh circuit</title>
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>V_{OUT}</m> in the circuit shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>A circuit used to demonstrate the voltage divider rule in a two-mesh circuit.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-voltagedividerexmeshesfig1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes containing one voltage source Vs and four resistors (R1, R2, R3, and R4). The high-potential end of Vs is connected to R1 at node a. R1, R2, and R3 are connected at node b. R3 and R4 are connected at node c. The low-potential end of Vs, R2, and R4 are connected at node d. Voltage Vx is measured over R2 from node b to node d. Voltage Vout is measured over R4 from node c to node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]					(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, v^=$V_X$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_3$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_4$, v^=$V_{OUT}$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The voltage divider rule cannot immediately be applied to calculate <m>V_{OUT}</m>. This is because the resistors are not all in series with the voltage source <m>V_S</m>. However, the voltage divider rule can be applied directly to calculate <m>V_X</m>, provided that the equivalent resistance seen by <m>R_2</m> is calculated. This creates a circuit with two resistances in series with the voltage source.</p>

					<p>The equivalent resistance seen by <m>R_2</m> is equal to</p>

					<p>
						<md>R_2 // (R_3 + R_4).</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now, the voltage divider rule can be used to calculate <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>V_X = V_S\left(\frac{R_2 // (R_3 + R_4)}{R_1 + (R_2 // (R_3 + R_4))}\right)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now that <m>V_X</m> is known, the circuit can be re-drawn to represent <m>V_X</m> as a source in series with resistors <m>R_3</m> and <m>R_4</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>The circuit with <m>V_X</m> represented as a source.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch2-voltagedividerexmeshesfig2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with one mesh and three nodes containing a voltage source Vx and two resistors (R3 and R4). The high-potential end of Vx connects to R3 at node a. R3 and R4 connect at node b. The low-potential end of Vx connects to R4 at node c. Voltage Vout is measured over R4 from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_X$]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_3$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_4$, v^=$V_{OUT}$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]					(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate the output voltage.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{OUT} \amp= V_X\left(\frac{R_4}{R_3 + R_4}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= V_S\left(\frac{R_2 // (R_3 + R_4)}{R_1 + (R_2 // (R_3 + R_4))}\right)\left(\frac{R_4}{R_3 + R_4}\right)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This process can be repeatedly iterated if there are three or more meshes in a circuit.</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

		</introduction>

		<subsection xml:id="ch2-wheatstone">
			<title>Wheatstone Bridge</title>

			<p>A Wheatstone bridge is a measuring tool that can be used to indirectly determine the resistance of an object with otherwise unknown resistance. It is useful when measuring small resistances (or changes in resistance) that may not be within the accuracy of an ohmmeter. As will be demonstrated, the unknown resistance value is largely invariant to noise from the voltage source as its value is independent from the source. The schematic of a Wheatstone bridge is shown in <xref ref="wheatstonebridgefig" />, with the unknown resistance labeled <m>R_X</m>.</p>

			<figure xml:id="wheatstonebridgefig">
  				<caption>Circuit schematic of a Wheatstone bridge circuit. <m>R_2</m> is a variable resistor and <m>R_X</m> is unknown.</caption>
  				<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-wheatstonebridgefig">
    				<shortdescription>Circuit with Vs, R1, R2, R3, Rx, and a voltmeter. Circuit has three meshes and four nodes. High potential of Vs, R1, and R3 connect at node a. R1, R2, and voltmeter connect at node b. R3, Rx, and voltmeter connect at node c. Low potential of Vs, R2, and Rx connect at node d. Voltage V1 is defined as high potential at node b and low potential at node d. V2 is defined as high potential at node c and low potential at node d.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
							\draw[]	(7,4) to [R, l^=$R_3$, -*]				(7,2);
							\draw[]	(4.5,4) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]			(4.5,2);
							\draw[]	(4.5,2) to [vR, l_=$R_2$, -*, v^=$V_1$]	(4.5,0);
							\draw[]	(4.5,2) to [rmeter, t=V]				(7,2);
							\draw[]	(7,2) to [R, l^=$R_X$, v_=$V_2$]		(7,0);
							\draw[]	(2,4) to [short]						(7,4);
							\draw[]	(2,4) to [V, l_=$V_S$]					(2,0);
							\draw[]	(2,0) to [short]						(7,0);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
  				</image>
			</figure>

			<p>The value of <m>R_X</m> can be determined if the bridge is balanced. This occurs when the value of the variable resistor (<m>R_2</m>) is changed such that the voltmeter reads 0<nbsp />V. When the bridge is balanced, <m>V_1</m> and <m>V_2</m> are equal. The voltage divider rule can be used to calculate <m>V_1</m> and <m>V_2</m>. Then, set <m>V_1</m> and <m>V_2</m> equal to calculate <m>R_X</m>. This is shown in <xref ref="wheatstoneeq" /> and derived in <xref ref="wheatstoneproof" />.</p>

			<p>
				<md number="yes" xml:id="wheatstoneeq">R_X = \frac{R_2R_3}{R_1}</md>
			</p>

			<proof xml:id="wheatstoneproof">
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>V_1 \amp= V_S\left(	\frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}	\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>V_2 \amp= V_S\left(	\frac{R_X}{R_3+R_X}	\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>V_1 \amp= V_2</mrow>
						<mrow>V_S\left(	\frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}	\right) \amp= V_S\left(	\frac{R_X}{R_3+R_X}	\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\frac{R_2}{R_1+R_2}	\amp=	\frac{R_X}{R_3+R_X}</mrow>
						<mrow>R_2(R_3+R_X) \amp= R_X(R_1+R_2)</mrow>
						<mrow>R_2R_3+R_2R_X \amp= R_XR_1 + R_XR_2</mrow>
						<mrow>R_2R_3 \amp= R_XR_1</mrow>
						<mrow>R_X \amp= \frac{R_2R_3}{R_1}</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
			</proof>

		</subsection>

  	</section>

    <section xml:id="ch2-currentdivider">
    	<title>Current Divider</title>

		<p>The current divider uses Ohm's law to calculate the current through resistive elements that are in parallel with each other without having to calculate voltage.</p>

		<p>In the simplest case, consider a circuit containing a current source (<m>I_S</m>) and two resistors (<m>R_1</m> and <m>R_2</m>), all wired in parallel. Ohm's law can be used to calculate the overall voltage/current relationship of the circuit, and is shown in <xref ref="currentdivtotaleq" />. (We are calculating voltage here only to show how to derive the current divider rule.)</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="currentdivtotaleq">V = I_S\left( \frac{1}{\frac{1}{R_1}+\frac{1}{R_2}} \right)</md>
		</p>

		<p>Because the voltage dropped over both resistors is equal to <m>V</m>, Ohm's law applied over each individual resistor can now be used to determine the individual currents through each resistor. Ohm's law states that the current through <m>R_1</m> is equal to <m>V/R_1</m>. The current through <m>R_2</m> is equal to <m>V/R_2</m>. Therefore, <xref ref="currentdivtotaleq" /> can be used to calculate the current through each resistor. This is shown in <xref ref="currentdivr1eq" /> and <xref ref="currentdivr2eq" />.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes">
				<mrow xml:id="currentdivr1eq">I_1 \amp= I_S\left( \frac{\frac{1}{R_1}}{\frac{1}{R_1}+\frac{1}{R_2}} \right)</mrow>
				<mrow xml:id="currentdivr2eq"> I_2 \amp= I_S\left( \frac{\frac{1}{R_2}}{\frac{1}{R_1}+\frac{1}{R_2}} \right)</mrow>
			</md>
		</p>

		<p>Note the similarity with the voltage divider rule. Here we are using reciprocal resistances due to the parallel configuration of the resistors. In general, <xref ref="currentdividereq" /> (derived below in <xref ref="currentdividerproof" />) can be used to calculate the current through the <m>k^\textrm{th}</m> resistor of <m>n</m> resistors in parallel (where <m>k \lt n</m>), given a source current of <m>I_S</m>. As mentioned, there is no need to calculate the total voltage as long as the current and resistor values are known.</p>

		<p>
			<md number="yes" xml:id="currentdividereq">I_k = I_S \left( \frac{R_{EQ}}{R_k} \right)</md>
		</p>

		<proof xml:id="currentdividerproof">
			<p>
				<md>
					<mrow>I_k \amp= I_S \left( \frac{\frac{1}{R_k}}{\frac{1}{R_1} + \frac{1}{R_2} + \frac{1}{R_3} + ... + \frac{1}{R_k} + ... + \frac{1}{R_n}} \right)</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp= I_S \left( \frac{\frac{1}{R_k}}{\frac{1}{R_{EQ}}} \right)</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp= I_S \left( \frac{R_{EQ}}{R_k} \right)</mrow>
				</md>
			</p>
		</proof>

		<example>
			<title>Using the current divider rule</title>

			<statement>
				<p>The current through the load resistor (<m>R_{LOAD}</m>) shown in the circuit below must be 50<nbsp />mA. Calculate the value of <m>R_{LOAD}</m> that is required to accomplish this.</p>

				<figure>
  						<caption>A circuit used to demonstrate the current divider rule.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-currentdividerex1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 300mA source, 2k, 3k, and Rload resistors. All four circuit elements are in parallel. Node a is "up" and node b is "down." The source flows "up." The current through Rload is defined as flowing "down."</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,0) to [I, l^=300~mA] (0,2.5);
									\draw (0,2.5) to [short] (7.5,2.5);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (7.5,0);
									\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, *-*] (2.5,0);
									\draw (5,2.5) to [R, l_=3~k$\Omega$, *-*] (5,0);
									\draw (7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_{LOAD}$, i^=$I_{LOAD}$] (7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Reduce the circuit by combining the 2<nbsp />k&#8486; and 3<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors in parallel. The circuit can be re-drawn.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>A circuit used to demonstrate the current divider rule with the known resistors reduced to an equivalent resistance.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-currentdividerex2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 300mA source, 1.2k, and Rload resistors. All three circuit elements are in parallel. Node a is "up" and node b is "down." The source flows "up." The current through Rload is defined as flowing "down."</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,0) to [I, l^=300~mA] (0,2.5);
									\draw (0,2.5) to [short] (5,2.5);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (5,0);
									\draw (2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=1.2~k$\Omega$, *-*] (2.5,0);
									\draw (5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_{LOAD}$, i^=50~mA] (5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate the load resistance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>50~\textrm{mA}	\amp=	300~\textrm{mA}\left(	\frac{\frac{(1.2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(R_{LOAD})}{(1.2~\textrm{k}\Omega+R_{LOAD})}}{R_{LOAD}}	\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{50~\textrm{mA}}{300~\textrm{mA}}	\amp=	\left(	\frac{1.2~\textrm{k}\Omega}{1.2~\textrm{k}\Omega+R_{LOAD}}	\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>1.2~\textrm{k}\Omega	\amp=	0.167 (1.2~\textrm{k}\Omega+R_{LOAD})</mrow>
							<mrow>7.2~\textrm{k}\Omega	\amp=	1.2~\textrm{k}\Omega + R_{LOAD}</mrow>
							<mrow>R_{LOAD}	\amp=	6~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
			</statement>
		</example>

		<example xml:id="ch2-currentdivider-threemesh-example">
				<title>Current divider with a three-mesh circuit</title>
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>I_{OUT}</m> in the circuit shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>A circuit used to demonstrate the current divider rule in a three-mesh circuit.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-currentdividerexmeshesfig1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 3 nodes consisting of a current source Is and four resistors (R1, R2, R3, and R4). Is, R1, and R2 connect at node a. Is flows into node a. R2, R3, and R4 connect at node b. Is, R1, R3, and R4 connect at node c. Is flows out of node c. Branch current Ix flows through R2 from node a to node b. Branch current Iout flows through R4 from node b to node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$]				(-2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]					(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_1$, *-*]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$, i^=$I_X$]		(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_3$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_4$, i_=$I_{OUT}$] 	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]						(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The current divider rule cannot immediately be applied to calculate <m>I_{OUT}</m>. This is because the resistors are not all in parallel with the current source. However, the current divider rule can be applied directly to calculate <m>I_X</m>, provided that the equivalent resistance seen by <m>R_1</m> is calculated. This creates a circuit with two resistances in parallel with the current source.</p>

					<p>The equivalent resistance seen by <m>R_1</m> is equal to</p>

					<p>
						<md>R_2 + (R_3 // R_4).</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now, the current divider rule can be used to calculate <m>I_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>I_X = I_S\left(\frac{R_1 // (R_2 + (R_3 // R_4))}{R_2+(R_3//R_4)}\right)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now that <m>I_X</m> is known, the circuit can be re-drawn to represent <m>I_X</m> as a source in parallel with resistors <m>R_3</m> and <m>R_4</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>The circuit with <m>I_X</m> represented as a source.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-currentdividerexmeshesfig2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two nodes and two meshes consisting of a current source Ix and resistors R3 and R4. All three circuit elements are connected in parallel between nodes a and b. Current source Ix flows out of node b into node a. Branch current Iout flows through R4 from node a to node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=$I_X$]					(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_3$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_4$, i_=$I_{OUT}$] 	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate the output voltage.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{OUT} \amp= I_X\left(\frac{R_3//R_4}{R_4}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= I_S\left(\frac{R_1 // (R_2 + (R_3 // R_4))}{R_2+(R_3//R_4)}\right)\left(\frac{R_3//R_4}{R_4}\right)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This process can be repeatedly iterated if there are four or more meshes in a circuit.</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch2-kirchhoffs">
		<title>Kirchhoff's Laws</title>

		<introduction>

			<p>Kirchhoff's laws, used together with Ohm's law, form a powerful set of tools for analyzing the currents through and voltages dropped over elements in circuits, even very complicated circuits. The two Kirchhoff's laws are Kirchhoff's current law (KCL) and Kirchhoff's voltage law (KVL).</p>

		</introduction>
	
		<subsection xml:id="ch2-kcl">
			<title>Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL)</title>

			<p>Kirchhoff's current law (KCL) is a byproduct of the principle of conservation of charge. At any point in a circuit, the charge flowing in to that point must be equal to the charge flowing out of that point. This relates to current because current is equal to the change in charge over time. If net charge is conserved, then net current must be as well. KCL can be stated in three slightly different, but identical, ways.</p>

			<p>
				<ul>
					<li><p>The sum of all current entering a node is equal to zero.</p></li>
					<li><p>The sum of all current leaving a node is equal to zero.</p></li>
					<li><p>The sum of all current entering a node is equal to the sum of all current leaving a node.</p></li>
				</ul>
			</p>

			<p>An important consequence of KCL is that the current through any branch of a circuit is the same everywhere throughout that branch at any moment in time. The currents that are calculated using KCL are called branch currents. (This may seem like the only type of current that can be measured, but we will learn later in this chapter that mesh analysis is a tool that enables us to find mesh currents, which are different from branch currents.)</p>

			<example>
				<title>Kirchhoff's current law</title>

				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the value of <m>I_2</m> in the circuit shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>A circuit used to demonstrate KCL.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-kclexample1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 200mA source, unknown element 1, unknown element 2, unknown element 3, and 100ohm resistor. Circuit has three meshes and three nodes. 200mA, unknown 1, unknown 2, and unknown 3 all connect at node a. The current flows into node a. Unknown 3 and 100ohm connect at node b. 200mA, unknown 1, unknown 2, and 100 ohm connect at node c. Output voltage of 10 V is defined as dropping between b and c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,0) to [I, l^=200~mA] (0,2.5);
									\draw (0,2.5) to [short] (5,2.5);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (7.5,0);
									\draw (2.5,2.5) to [generic, i_=50~mA, *-*] (2.5,0);
									\draw (5,2.5) to [generic, i_=I\textsubscript{2}, *-*] (5,0);
									\draw (5,2.5) to [generic] (7.5,2.5);
									\draw (7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, v^=10~V] (7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>KCL states that the sum of all currents entering the node connecting all three generic elements to the high potential end of the current source must be zero. Use this and Ohm's law to solve for <m>I_2</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0	\amp=	200~\textrm{mA} - 50~\textrm{mA} - I_2 - \frac{10~\textrm{V}}{0.1~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>I_2	\amp=	50~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch2-kvl">
			<title>Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL)</title>

			<p>Kirchhoff's voltage law (KVL) is a byproduct of the principle of conservation of energy. Energy must be conserved in each loop in a circuit. Energy is equal to charge times voltage, and as energy and charge are both conserved, voltage must be as well. KVL can be stated in three slightly different, but identical, ways.</p>

			<p>
				<ul>
					<li><p>The sum of all of the voltage drops in a loop is equal to zero.</p></li>
					<li><p>The sum of all of the voltage rises in a loop is equal to zero.</p></li>
					<li><p>The sum of all of the voltage drops in a loop is equal to the sum of all of the voltage rises in a loop.</p></li>
				</ul>
			</p>

			<p>An important consequence of KVL is that the voltage drops over parallel elements are identical.</p>

			<example>
				<title>Kirchhoff's voltage law</title>

				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the value of the voltage drop over each of the three labeled circuit elements in the circuit below. Indicate the correct polarity.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>A circuit used to demonstrate KVL.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-kvlexample1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit containing 3V source, 2V source, 5V source, element 1, element 2, and element 3. Circuit has three meshes and four nodes. High potential of 3V, element 1, and element 3 connect at a. Low potential of 2V, element 1, and element 2 connect at b. High potential of 5V, element 2, and element 3 connect at c. Low potential of 3V, high potential of 2V, and low potential of 5V connect at node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,2.5) to [V, l_=3~V] (0,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (5,0);
									\draw (0,2.5) to [generic, l^=element 1, *-*] (2.5,2.5);
									\draw (2.5,0) to [V, l^=2~V, *-] (2.5,2.5);
									\draw (2.55,2.5) to [generic, l^=element 2, -*] (5,2.5);
									\draw (5,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V] (5,0);
									\draw (0,4) to [generic, l^=element 3] (5,4);
									\draw (0,2.5) to [short] ++(up:1.5);
									\draw (5,2.5) to [short] ++(up:1.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>KVL states that the sum of all voltage drops in a loop must be equal to zero. The lower left loop can be analyzed in a clockwise fashion to calculate the voltage drop over element 1, assuming the high-potential end of <m>V_1</m> is on the left. The positive result indicates that the left-hand side of the element is indeed the high-potential side.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= -3~\textrm{V} + V_1 - 2~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>V_1 \amp= 5~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The lower right loop can be analyzed in a clockwise fashion to calculate the voltage drop over element 2, assuming the high-potential end of <m>V_2</m> is on the left. The negative result indicates that the right-hand side of the element is the high-potential side.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= 2~\textrm{V} + V_2 + 5~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>V_2 \amp= -7~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The loop consisting of the 3<nbsp />V source, element 3, and the 5<nbsp />V source can be analyzed in a clockwise fashion to calculate the voltage drop over element 3, assuming the high-potential end of <m>V_3</m> is on the left. The negative resykt indicates that the right-hand side of the element is the high-potential side.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= -3~\textrm{V} + V_3 + 5~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>V_3 \amp= -2~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The circuit can now be re-drawn, indicating the voltage and polarity of each circuit element.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>A circuit used to demonstrate KVL, with all voltages labeled.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-kvlexample2">
    						<shortdescription>Same circuit as above. Element 1 has voltage drop of 5V dropping from a to b. Element 2 has voltage drop of 7V dropping from c to b. Element 3 has voltage drop of 2V dropping from c to a.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw (0,2.5) to [V, l_=3~V] (0,0);
									\draw (0,0) to [short] (5,0);
									\draw (0,2.5) to [generic, l_=element 1, *-*, v^=5~V] (2.5,2.5);
									\draw (2.5,0) to [V, l^=2~V, *-] (2.5,2.5);
									\draw (5,2.5) to [generic, l^=element 2, *-, v_=7~V] (2.5,2.5);
									\draw (5,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V] (5,0);
									\draw (5,4) to [generic, l^=element 3, v_=2~V] (0,4);
									\draw (0,2.5) to [short] ++(up:1.5);
									\draw (5,2.5) to [short] ++(up:1.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch2-matrixequations">
			<title>Deriving Matrix Equations from KCL and KVL</title>

			<p>Together, KCL and KVL can be used to solve for every branch current in a circuit. Those branch currents can be multiplied by resistance to calculate voltage drops over each resistor in a circuit. By analyzing nodes and loops in a circuit, a number of equations (using KCL, KVL, and Ohm's law) can be found. If there are <m>n</m> unknown currents in the circuit, <m>n</m> linearly independent equations will be needed. A matrix can then be used to solve for the unknowns.</p>

			<p>This book will not discuss matrix reduction techniques, which is better suited for a linear algebra textbook. Gauss-Jordan elimination can be used to find the reduced row echelon form of a matrix. Alternatively, a graphing calculator can be used to solve the matrices. In short: this book is not about how to solve matrix equations. This book is about how to set up those matrix equations by using KCL, KVL, and Ohm's law.</p>

			<p>This textbook will use terminology that may not be standard to demonstrate the ideal nodes to use to calculate KCL and the ideal loops to use to calculate KVL. Ideal nodes to use with KCL will be referred to as <term>perfect nodes.</term> A perfect node denotes a node at which we know or care about all of the currents entering or exiting that node. Ideal loops to use with KVL will be referred to as <term>perfect loops.</term> In a perfect loop, all of the voltages are known or are known resistances multiplied by currents we either know or intend to calculate with KCL.</p>

			<p>To derive a sufficient number of <term>linearly independent</term> equations, each loop must contain at least one circuit element that is unique from all other loops. Each node must contain at least one branch current that is unique from the other nodes.</p>

			<p>It is not always possible to identify a sufficient number of perfect loops and perfect nodes. If there are more unknowns than equations after exhausting all of the perfect nodes and loops, then it is time to define a new unknown. Then, more node or loop equations will be available to use.</p>

			<example>
				<title>Demonstrating using KCL and KVL to form a matrix of equations</title>

				<statement>
					<p>Consider the circuit shown in <xref ref="kclkvlexfig" />. There are four unknown currents, so four linearly independent equations will need to be derived to solve for each current. The direction of each current has been arbitrarily chosen.</p>

					<figure xml:id="kclkvlexfig">
  						<caption>Circuit used to demonstrate using KCL and KVL to solve for unknown currents.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-kclkvlexfig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with Vs, Is, R1, R2, R3, and R4. Circuit has three meshes and four nodes. High potential of Vs, R1, and R2 connect at a. Unknown current I1 flows out of a. Unknown current I2 flows out of a. R2, R3, and R4 connect at b. Unknown current I2 flows into b. Unknown current I3 flows out of b. Unknown current I4 flows out of b. R1, R4, and Is connect at c. Unknown current I1 flows into c. Unknown current I4 flows into c. Is flows out of c. Low potential of Vs, R3, and Is connect at d. Unknown current I3 flows into d. Is flows into d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[above] {b} to [R, l^=$R_3$, i_=$I_3$, *-*](0,0) node[ground] {} node[above right] {d};
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_4$, i_=$I_4$, -*]			(2.5,2.5) node[right] {c};
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) node[left] {a} to [R, l^=$R_2$, i_=$I_2$, *-]	(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]						(-2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,4) to [R, l^=$R_1$, i_=$I_1$]				(2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]							(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short]							(-2.5,4);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4) to [short]							(2.5,2);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [I, l^=$I_S$]						(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>To find a sufficient number of linearly independent equations, analysis can occur at nodes (using KCL) and around loops (using KVL). It is important to try to find nodes at which we know (or want to know) all of the currents entering and exiting. In <xref ref="kclkvlexfig" />, there are four nodes, each labeled with a letter. This means we could generate four KCL equations (one at each node). However, by closely analyzing nodes a and d, we see that the current through the voltage source is unknown. Therefore, it would not be ideal to include nodes a or d in our set of equations. These are not perfect nodes.</p>

					<p>The circuit in <xref ref="kclkvlexfig" /> therefore has two perfect nodes: node b and node c. These nodes will be used solve KCL, giving us our first two linearly independent equations, shown in <xref ref="kclequations1aeq" /> and <xref ref="kclequations1beq" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow xml:id="kclequations1aeq">I_2-I_3-I_4 \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="kclequations1beq">I_1+I_4-I_S \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>We turn to KVL to find the remaining two equations. Just as we searched for perfect nodes to derive KCL equations, we will look for perfect loops to derive KVL equations. In <xref ref="kclkvlexfig" />, any loop that contains the current source would not be a perfect loop; we do not know (or care to find) the voltage dropped over the current source.</p>

					<p>The loops that we choose must contain at least one unique circuit element from the others. For example (which is not necessarily relevant to the solution of this particular circuit): the loop containing <m>V_S</m>, <m>R_2</m>, and <m>R_3</m> as well as the loop containing <m>R_3</m>, <m>R_4</m>, and <m>I_S</m> are linearly independent. Including a third loop of <m>V_S</m>, <m>R_2</m>, <m>R_4</m>, and <m>I_S</m> would not add anything new to the circuit. The equations derived from these three loops would not lead to three linearly independent equations; they would only lead to two linearly independent equations.</p>

					<p>Turning back to the example at hand: we desire two linearly independent equations that come from perfect loops. Two loops that satisfy those criteria are: the loop containing <m>V_S</m>, <m>R_2</m>, and <m>R_3</m> and the loop containing <m>R_1</m>, <m>R_4</m>, and <m>R_2</m>. Apply KVL around each loop. Use the direction of the current to determine if the voltage is a rise or a drop. Voltage rises will be negative, and voltage drops will be positive. The two equations are given in <xref ref="kvlequations1aeq" /> and <xref ref="kvlequations1beq" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow xml:id="kvlequations1aeq">-V_S + R_2I_2+R_3I_3 \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="kvlequations1beq">R_1I_1 - R_4I_4 - R_2I_2 \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now that four linearly independent equations have been found, they can be put into the form of <m>\alpha I_1 + \beta I_2 + \gamma I_3 + \delta I_4 = c</m> and placed into a matrix. Each of the equations, rewritten, are shown in <xref ref="fourequationseq" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="fourequationseq">
							\begin{array}{r r r r l}
								\amp I_2 \amp- I_3 \amp- I_4 \amp= 0\\
								I_1 \amp \amp \amp+ I_4 \amp= I_S\\
								\amp R_2I_2	\amp+ R_3I_3 \amp \amp= V_S\\
								R_1I_1 \amp- R_2I_2	\amp \amp- R_4I_4 \amp= 0
							\end{array}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The corresponding matrix is shown in <xref ref="matrixkclkvleq" />. Now it can be solved to determine each of the individual currents.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="matrixkclkvleq">
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								0 	\amp 1 		\amp -1 		\amp -1 		\amp 0\\
								1 	\amp 0 		\amp 0 		\amp 1 		\amp I_S\\
								0	\amp R_2	\amp R_3	\amp 0		\amp V_S\\
								R_1	\amp -R_2	\amp 0		\amp -R_4	\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>
		
	</section>

	<section xml:id="ch2-meshanalysis">
		<title>Mesh Analysis</title>

		<introduction>

			<p>Mesh analysis is another tool that uses KCL and KVL to solve for unknowns. In this case, <term>mesh currents</term> (as opposed to branch currents, which the previous analysis tools calculated) will be derived. In this book, mesh currents are defined in a clockwise direction.</p>

		</introduction>
	
		<subsection xml:id="ch2-branchvmesh">
			<title>Branch vs. Mesh Currents</title>

			<p>A <term>branch current</term> denotes how much current flows through each circuit element in a particular branch. This makes the concept of branch currents relatively easy to understand. Branch currents are something we could measure with an ammeter. On the other hand, a <term>mesh current</term> defines the current through an entire mesh, and is not necessarily equal to any particular branch current. A mesh current is more of an abstraction than a physical parameter.</p>
			
			<p>It is possible to derive a mathematical relationship between mesh and branch currents. When a branch current exists entirely within a single mesh, then that branch current is equal to that mesh current. (The sign will be negative if the mesh current is defined in the opposite direction as the branch current.) When a branch current is <q>shared</q> between two meshes, then the branch current is equal to the sum of the shared branch currents (paying close attention to the direction to determine the sign).</p>

			<example>
				<title>Branch and mesh currents in a circuit</title>

				<statement>
					<p>The circuit shown in <xref ref="meshvsbranchfig" /> has two meshes (and therefore two mesh currents: <m>I_A</m> and <m>I_B</m>). In addition, there are three branch currents defined as <m>I_1</m>, <m>I_2</m>, and <m>I_3</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="meshvsbranchfig">
  						<caption>This circuit contains two meshes and three defined branches.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-meshvsbranchfig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes consisting of Vs and resistors R1, R2, R3, and R4. The high-potential end of Vs connects to R1 at node A. Resistors R1, R2, and R3 connect at node B. Resistors R3 and R4 connect at node C. The low-potential end of Vs and resistors R2 and R4 connect at node D. Branch current I1 is defined as flowing through R1 from node A to node B. Branch current I2 is defined as flowing through R2 from node B to node C. Branch current I3 is defined as flowing through R3 from node B to node C. Mesh current Ia flows through Vs, R1, and R2 through nodes D, A, B, and D. Mesh current Ib flows through R2, R3, and R4 through nodes D, B, C, and D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$] (0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$, i^=$I_1$] (2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_2$, i^=$I_2$, *-*] (2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_3$, i^=$I_3$] (5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_4$] (5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short] (5,0);
									\draw[Stealth-] (1.2,1.25)node{$I_A$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (3.7,1.25)node{$I_B$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Each branch current is solved for as a function of the two mesh currents.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_1 \amp= I_A</mrow>
							<mrow>I_2 \amp= I_A-I_B</mrow>
							<mrow>I_3 \amp= I_B</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

		<subsection xml:id="ch2-meshanalysismethod">
			<title>The Mesh Analysis Method</title>

			<p>The steps to performing mesh analysis are given below.</p>

			<p>
				<ol>
					<li><p>Identify each mesh and draw and label each mesh (use a clockwise direction).</p></li>
					<li><p>Perform KVL around each mesh (using the mesh currents) and determine the corresponding equations.</p></li>
					<li><p>If a current source is contained within a mesh, that mesh current will be equal to the value of the current source.</p></li>
					<li>
						<p>If a current source is shared between two meshes:</p>
						<p>
							<ol>
								<li><p>pretend that the current source does not exist (treat it as an open circuit) and find the KVL equation for the loop connecting the two meshes (called a <term>supermesh</term>), then</p></li>
								<li><p>relate the two mesh currents in the supermesh to the current source (branch current) to obtain a new equation.</p></li>
							</ol>
						</p>
					</li>
					<li><p>If there is a dependent source (or sources), additional equations relating the controlling values may be required.</p></li>
				</ol>
			</p>

			<example>
				<title>Demonstrating mesh analysis</title>

				<statement>
					<p>The circuit shown below will be used to demonstrate the mesh analysis procedure. It has been carefully chosen to contain both a dependent source and a supermesh. Each of the meshes have already been identified and labeled.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit used to demonstrate the mesh analysis method.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-meshanalysisex1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 5 nodes consisting of rm*I CCVS, current source Is, and resistors R1, R2, R3, R4, and R5. Resistors R2 and R3 connect to Is at node A. Is flows into node A. The high-potential end of the CCVS connects to resistors R1 and R2 at node B. Resistors R1, R3, and R4 connect at node C. Is connects to resistors R4 and R5 at node D. Is flows out of node D. The low-potential end of the CCVS connects to resistor R5 at node E. Mesh current I1 flows through the CCVS, resistor R2, Is, and resistor R5 through nodes E, B, A, D, and E. Mesh current I2 flows through Is, resistor R3, and resistor R4 through nodes D, A, C, and D. Mesh current I3 flows through resistors R1, R3, and R2 through nodes B, C, A, and B. Branch current I is defined as flowing through R2 from node B to node A.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[] (-3,3) to [cV, l_=$r_mI$, *-] (-3,0);
									\draw[] (-3,3) to [R, l^=$R_2$, i^=$I$] (0,3);
									\draw[] (0,0) to [I, l^=$I_S$, *-*] (0,3) node[above] {a};
									\draw[] (0,3) to [R, l^=$R_3$, -*] (3,3);
									\draw[] (3,3) to [R, l^=$R_4$] (3,0);
									\draw[] (0,0) to [short] (3,0);
									\draw[] (-3,0) to [R, l^=$R_5$] (0,0);
									\draw[] (-3,3) to [short] (-3,5);
									\draw[] (3,3) to [short] (3,5);
									\draw[] (-3,5) to [R, l^=$R_1$] (3,5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (-1.5,1.5)node{$I_1$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (1.5,1.5)node{$I_2$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (0,4)node{$I_3$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>There is only one regular mesh in this circuit: the one containing mesh current <m>I_3</m>. The other two meshes correspond to a supermesh due to the current source that exists at the intersection of the two meshes. Therefore there is only one mesh equation to find, shown in <xref ref="mesh1eq" />. (Note that we are ignoring <m>I</m>, the branch current used to define the current-controlled voltage source.) This concludes step two.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="mesh1eq">R_1I_3 + R_3(I_3-I_2) + R_2(I_3-I_1) = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>Step three does not apply to this circuit. There are no current sources that are unshared between meshes. Step four introduces the supermesh. The supermesh consists of the two loops containing <m>I_1</m> and <m>I_2</m>. Because of the existence of the supermesh, KCL will be applied at node a. The supermesh equation is given in <xref ref="supermesh2eq" /> and the KCL equation is shown in <xref ref="mesh2kcleq" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes">
							<mrow xml:id="supermesh2eq">-r_mI + R_2(I_1-I_3) + R_3(I_2-I_3) + R_4I_2 + R_5I_1 \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow xml:id="mesh2kcleq">I_1 + I_S - I_2 \amp= 0</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>At this point, there are three equations. However, the presence of the controlling current (<m>I</m>) provides an additional unknown. This means that one more equation is required. We can use the relationship between branch currents and mesh currents to find an equation for the controlling current, shown in <xref ref="mesh4eq" />. While this equation can be directly plugged in to the supermesh equation <xref ref="supermesh2eq" />, it is my opinion that it eliminates error to include it as a separate equation to be used in the matrix.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="mesh4eq">I = I_1 - I_3</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now that there are four linearly independent equations (<xref ref="mesh1eq" />, <xref ref="supermesh2eq" />, <xref ref="mesh2kcleq" />, and <xref ref="mesh4eq" />), they can be rewritten in the form of <m>\alpha I_1 + \beta I_2 + \gamma I_3 + \delta I = c</m> and placed into a matrix. Each of the equations, rewritten, are shown in <xref ref="fourequationsmesheq" />.</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="fourequationsmesheq">
							\begin{array}{r r r r l}
								-R_2I_1	\amp- R_3I_2 \amp+ (R_1+R_2+R_3) I_3 \amp \amp= 0\\
								(R_2+R_5)I_1 \amp +(R_3+R_4)I_2	\amp-(R_2+R_3)I_3 \amp-r_mI	\amp= 0\\
								I_1	\amp -I_2 \amp \amp \amp= -I_S\\
								I_1	\amp \amp- I_3	\amp- I	\amp= 0
						\end{array}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The corresponding matrix is shown in <xref ref="matrixmesheq" />. It can be solved to find each of the mesh currents (as well as the controlling current).</p>

					<p>
						<md number="yes" xml:id="matrixmesheq">
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								-R_2 		\amp -R_3 		\amp (R_1+R_2+R_3)	\amp 0 		\amp 0\\
								(R_2+R_5) 	\amp (R_3+R_4)	\amp -(R_2+R_3) 		\amp -r_m 	\amp 0\\
								1			\amp -1			\amp 0				\amp 0		\amp -I_S\\
								1			\amp 0			\amp -1				\amp -1		\amp 0
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>
				</statement>
			</example>

		</subsection>

	</section>

	<exercises>
    	<title>Practice Problems</title>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Equivalent Resistance</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch2eqivresistanceq1">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance (as seen between nodes a and b) of the resistors shown in <xref ref="circuit21fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit21fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2eqivresistanceq1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit21fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with R1, R2, and R3. Circuit has three nodes. R1 connects at node a. R1, R2, and R3 connect at node c. R2 and R3 connect at node b. The equivalent resistance is measured between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[left] {a} to [R, l^=$R_1$, o-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]	(5,0.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,-0.5) to [R, l^=$R_3$]	(5,-0.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0.5) to [short]		(2.5,-0.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0.5) to [short]		(5,-0.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, *-o]		(5.5,0) node[right] {b};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							R_{EQ} = R_1 + \frac{R_2R_3}{R_2+R_3}
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Combine <m>R_2</m> and <m>R_3</m> in parallel. Then combine that result in series with <m>R_1</m>.</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2eqivresistanceq2">
				<statement>
					<p>Minimize the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="circuit22fig" /> as much as possible.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit22fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2eqivresistanceq2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit22fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 10ohm, 24ohm, 18ohm, 47ohm, 33ohm, 130mA source, and 2V source. Circuit has five nodes. 10ohm and 24ohm connect at node a. 24ohm, 18ohm, 47ohm, and 130mA connect at node b. 130mA flows out of node b. 18ohm and high potential of 2V connect at node c. 130mA, 47ohm, and 33ohm connect at node d. 130mA flows into node d. 10ohm, low potential of 2V, and 33ohm connect at node e.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,3) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,3) to [R, l^=24~$\Omega$]		(2.5,3);
									\draw[]	(2.5,3) to [R, l_=18~$\Omega$, *-]	(2.5,1.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,1.5) to [V, l_=2~V, -*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,3) to [R, l^=47~$\Omega$]		(5,3);
									\draw[]	(2.5,3) to [short]					(2.5,4.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4.5) to [I, l^=130~mA]			(5,4.5);
									\draw[]	(5,3) to [short]					(5,4.5);
									\draw[]	(5,3) to [R, l^=33~$\Omega$, *-]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<figure>
  						<caption>Reduced circuit diagram corresponding to the answer to <xref ref="ch2eqivresistanceq2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit22figanswer">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 34ohm, 18ohm, 47ohm, 33ohm, 130mA source, and 2V source. Circuit has four nodes. 34ohm, 18ohm, 47ohm, and 130mA connect at node a. 130mA flows out of node a. 18ohm and high potential of 2V connect at node b. 130mA, 47ohm, and 33ohm connect at node c. 130mA flows into node c. 34ohm, low potential of 2V, and 33ohm connect at node d.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,3) to [R, l_=34~$\Omega$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,3) to [short]					(2.5,3);
									\draw[]	(2.5,3) to [R, l_=18~$\Omega$, *-]	(2.5,1.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,1.5) to [V, l_=2~V, -*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,3) to [R, l^=47~$\Omega$]		(5,3);
									\draw[]	(2.5,3) to [short]					(2.5,4.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,4.5) to [I, l^=130~mA]			(5,4.5);
									\draw[]	(5,3) to [short]					(5,4.5);
									\draw[]	(5,3) to [R, l^=33~$\Omega$, *-]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The only circuit elements that can be combined in this circuit are the 10<nbsp />&#8486; and 24<nbsp />&#8486; resistors. These two resistors are in series, with an equivalent series resistance of 34<nbsp />&#8486;. No other resistors are purely in series or purely in parallel. The two DC sources cannot be combined, as they are two different types of sources and are additionally neither connected in series nor in parallel.</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2eqivresistanceq3">
				<statement>
					<p>Use the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="circuit23fig" /> to calculate the equivalent resistance between nodes a and b. Each resistor has a value of 1<nbsp />k&#8486;.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit23fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2eqivresistanceq3" /> and <xref ref="ch2eqivresistanceq4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit23fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 7 resistors (R1 through R7) and six nodes. R1 and R2 connect at node a. R2 and R3 connect at node d. R3, R4, and R5 connect at node e. R1, R4, and R7 connect at node f. R5 and R6 connect at node b. R6 and R7 connect at node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,1.5) node[left] {a} to [R, o-]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,1.5) to [R, o-]					(0,3);
									\draw[]	(0,3) to [R]						(2.5,3);
									\draw[]	(2.5,3) to [R, *-*]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,3) to [R, -o]					(5,3) node[right] {b};
									\draw[]	(5,3) to [R, o-o]					(5,0) node[right] {c};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [R, -o]					(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<hint>
					<p>Use either a delta-wye transform or a wye-delta transform.</p>
				</hint>
				
				<answer>
					<p>1.40<nbsp />k&#8486;</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Start by re-drawing the circuit to include resistor values and highlight nodes a and b for calculating the equivalent resistance. This makes it more clear which resistors can be combined in series and parallel. Note that one of the resistors has been re-drawn to be horizontal rather than vertical. This also makes series and parallel combinations stand out more clearly without modifying the circuit in any meaningful way.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Re-drawn circuit with resistor values, highlighting nodes a and b.</caption>
  						<image width="70%" xml:id="ch2-circuit23figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit is functionally equivalent to above, changes described in text.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [short, *-o]				(-2.5,3.5) node[above] {a};
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-o]					(5,3.5) node[above] {b};
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]		(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]		(-2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]			(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,0) to [short]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]			(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>There are two sets of series resistances. Combine all series resistors and re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Re-drawn circuit with series resistances reduced.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-circuit23figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 resistors (R1, R4, and R5 same as before and new resistor Rx=R2+R3 and Ry=R6+R7) and four nodes. Nodes are labeled same as previous. R1 and Rx connect at node a. Rx, R4, and R5 connect at node e. R1, R4, and Ry connect at node f. R5 and Ry connect at node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, *-o]					(0,3.5) node[above] {a};
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-o]					(5,3.5) node[above] {b};
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$]			(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Either a wye-delta transform or a delta-wye transform can be performed to solve this circuit. The following solution utilizes a wye-delta transform. The wye circuit elements are indicated in red.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit with wye resistances indicated in red.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-circuit23figans3">
    						<shortdescription>Same circuit as previous. 2k resistor between a and e is red. R4 is red. R5 is red.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]		(0,2.5) to [short, *-o]				(0,3.5) node[above] {a};
									\draw[]		(5,2.5) to [short, *-o]				(5,3.5) node[above] {b};
									\draw[]		(0,2.5) node[left, red] {A} to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]	(0,0);
									\draw[red]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$, color=red]	(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[red]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*, color=red](2.5,0) node[below] {C};
									\draw[red]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$, color=red]	(5,2.5) node[right] {B};
									\draw[]		(5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]		(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate each of the delta resistances.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_1	\amp=	\frac{(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)+(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)+(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= 5~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>R_2	\amp=	\frac{(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)+(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)+(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= 2.5~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>R_3	\amp=	\frac{(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)+(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)+(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= 5~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit after the wye-delta transform.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-circuit23figans4">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit from before now has a new label for node c. Circuit has 3 nodes. 1k (R1), one 5k, and another 5k connect at node a. The other 5k, 2.5k, 2k (Ry) connect at node b. 1k (R1), one 5k, 2.5k, and 2k (Ry) connect at node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, *-o]				(0,3.5) node[above] {a};
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-o]				(5,3.5) node[above] {b};
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) node[left] {A} to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=5~k$\Omega$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=5~k$\Omega$, -*]	(2.5,0) node[below] {C};
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [R, l_=2.5~k$\Omega$]	(5,2.5) node[right] {B};
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
					
					<p>Between nodes A and C, the 1<nbsp />k&#8486; and 5<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors are in parallel. They have an equivalent resistance of 0.83<nbsp />k&#8486;. Between nodes B and C, the 2.5<nbsp />k&#8486; and 2<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors are in parallel. They have an equivalent resistance of 1.11<nbsp />k&#8486;. The circuit can be re-drawn again.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit after the wye-delta transform and reduction of parallel resistances.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit23figans5">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with three nodes now with only three resistors. 0.833k and 5k connect at node a. 5k and 1.11k connect at node b. 0.833k and 1.11k connect at node c.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, *-o]				(0,3.5) node[above] {a};
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-o]				(5,3.5) node[above] {b};
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=0.833~k$\Omega$]	(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=5~k$\Omega$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=1.11~k$\Omega$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Between nodes a and b, the 0.833<nbsp />k&#8486; and 1.11<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors are in series. They have an equivalent resistance of 1.943<nbsp />k&#8486;. The circuit can be re-drawn again.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit after the wye-delta transform and reduction of parallel and series resistances.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-circuit23figans6">
    						<shortdescription>5k and 1.94k resistors are in parallel between nodes a and b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short, *-o]				(0,3.5) node[above] {a};
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-o]				(5,3.5) node[above] {b};
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=5~k$\Omega$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]				(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [R, l^=1.943~k$\Omega$]	(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance between nodes a and b is the parallel combination of the two remaining resistances.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ}	\amp= 1.943~\textrm{k}\Omega//5~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{(1.943~\textrm{k}\Omega)(5~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{1.943~\textrm{k}\Omega + 5~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.40~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This will not be solved using delta-wye transforms. Do this as a practice exercise. The equivalent resistance calculation should be 1.40<nbsp />k&#8486; regardless of the method used to calculate it. As a hint, the following circuit diagrams show the two possible delta configurations that can be used in a delta-wye transform. The delta resistances are highlighted in red.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit highlighting one possible delta configuration.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-circuit23figansdelta1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 resistors (R1, R4, and R5 same as before and new resistor Rx=R2+R3 and Ry=R6+r7) and four nodes. Nodes are labeled same as at start of this question. R1 (red) and Rx (red) connect at node a. Rx (red), R4 (red), and R5 connect at node e. R1 (red), R4 (red), and Ry connect at node f. R5 and Ry connect at node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]		(0,2.5) to [short, *-o]	(0,3.5) node[above] {a};
									\draw[]		(5,2.5) to [short, *-o]	(5,3.5) node[above] {b};
									\draw[red]	(0,2.5) node[left] {A} to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, color=red](0,0);
									\draw[red]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$, color=red](2.5,2.5) node[above] {B};
									\draw[red]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*, color=red](2.5,0) node[below] {C};
									\draw[]		(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]		(5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$]					(5,0);
									\draw[red]		(0,0) to [short, color=red]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]		(2.5,0) to [short]							(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit highlighting another possible delta configuration.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-circuit23figansdelta2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 5 resistors (R1, R4, and R5 same as before and new resistor Rx=R2+R3 and Ry=R6+r7) and four nodes. Nodes are labeled same as at start of this question. R1 and Rx connect at node a. Rx, R4 (red), and R5 (red) connect at node e. R1, R4 (red), and Ry (red) connect at node f. R5 (red) and Ry (red) connect at node b.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]		(0,2.5) to [short, *-o]		(0,3.5) node[above] {a};
									\draw[]		(5,2.5) to [short, *-o]		(5,3.5) node[above] {b};
									\draw[]		(0,2.5) node[left] {A} to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]		(0,0);
									\draw[]		(0,2.5) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[red]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*, color=red]	(2.5,0) node[below] {C};
									\draw[red]	(2.5,2.5) node[above] {A} to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$, color=red]	(5,2.5) node[right] {B};
									\draw[red]		(5,2.5) to [R, l_=2~k$\Omega$, color=red]			(5,0);
									\draw[]		(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
									\draw[]		(0,0) to [short]								(2.5,0);
									\draw[red]		(2.5,0) to [short, color=red]					(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2eqivresistanceq4">
				<statement>
					<p>Use the circuit diagram shown in <xref ref="circuit23fig" /> to calculate the equivalent resistance between nodes b and c. Each resistor has a value of 1<nbsp />k&#8486;.</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>0.733<nbsp />k&#8486;</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Re-draw the circuit to include the resistor values as well as to emphasize the two nodes to calculate equivalent resistance between.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Re-drawn circuit with resistor values, highlighting nodes b and c.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit24figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit is functionally equivalent to previous question figure, changes described in text.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,3) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]			(0,1.5);
									\draw[]	(0,1.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,3) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]			(2.5,3);
									\draw[]	(2.5,3) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,3) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]			(5,3);
									\draw[]	(5,3) to [short, *-o]					(6,3) node[right] {b};
									\draw[]	(5,3) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, *-o]					(6,0) node[right] {c};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]			(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Start by combining all series resistors together, then re-draw the circuit. There is only one set of three resistors in series, all the way on the left-hand side of the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Re-drawn circuit with series resistors combined.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-circuit24figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit has two meshes. Left mesh contains a 3k and one 1k resistor. Right mesh shares the 1k resistor and has three additional 1k resistors. Nodes b and c defined between one of these 1k resistors.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l_=3~k$\Omega$]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-o]					(6,2.5) node[right] {b};
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, *-o]					(6,0) node[right] {c};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]			(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Combine the 3<nbsp />k&#8486; and 1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors that are in parallel with each other. These are the only two parallel resistors in the circuit at this point. The parallel resistance of these two resistances is 0.75<nbsp />k&#8486;. Re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Re-drawn circuit with parallel resistors combined.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-circuit24figans3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit has one mesh. The mesh contains a 0.75k and three 1k resistors. Nodes b and c defined between one of these 1k resistors.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=0.75~k$\Omega$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-o]					(6,2.5) node[right] {b};
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, *-o]					(6,0) node[right] {c};
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]			(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Now there are three resistors in series, two of the 1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors and the 0.75<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor. The series resistance is equal to 2.75<nbsp />k&#8486;. Re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Re-drawn circuit with series resistors combined.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-circuit24figans4">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit has one mesh. The mesh contains a 2.75k and one 1k resistor. Nodes b and c defined between both of the resistors.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=2.75~k$\Omega$]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short, *-o]					(6,2.5) node[right] {b};
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [short, *-o]					(6,0) node[right] {c};
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [short]						(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance is equal to the parallel combination of the remaining 1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor and the 2.75<nbsp />k&#8486; resistance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 2.75~\textrm{k}\Omega//1~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{(2.75~\textrm{k}\Omega)(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{2.75~\textrm{k}\Omega+1~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.733~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2eqivresistanceq5">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the equivalent resistance (between nodes a and b) of the resistors shown in <xref ref="circuit25fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit25fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2eqivresistanceq5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit25fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 10k, 1.8k, 24k, 4.7k, 2k, 5.6k, 18k, and 6.8k resistors and seven nodes. 10k, 1.8k, and 4.7k connect at node b. 1.8k, 2k, and 24k connect at node a. 24k and 5.6k connect at node c. 4.7k and 18k connect at node d. 2k, 18k, and 6.8k connect at node e. 6.8k and 5.6k connect at node f. 10k connects at g.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) node[left] {a} to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$, o-]		(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l^=4.7~k$\Omega$]		(5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=1.8~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=24~k$\Omega$, -*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l^=18~k$\Omega$, -*]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=6.8~k$\Omega$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) node[left] {b} to [short, o-]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,0) to [R, l^=5.6~k$\Omega$]		(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</statement>

				<hint>
					<p>Use either a delta-wye transform or a wye-delta transform.</p>
				</hint>

				<answer>
					<p>20.4<nbsp />k&#8486;</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>There are two sets of series resistors that can be combined. The first set is the 4.7<nbsp />k&#8486; and 18<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors, with an equivalent series resistance of 22.7<nbsp />k&#8486;. The second set is the 6.8<nbsp />k&#8486; and 5.6<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors, with an equivalent series resistance of 12.4<nbsp />k&#8486;. The circuit can be re-drawn.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit re-drawn with series resistances combined.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit25figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 10k, 1.8k, 24k, 22.7k, 2k, and 12.4k resistors and five nodes. Nodes are labeled same as previous. 10k, 1.8k, and 22.7k connect at node b. 1.8k, 2k, and 24k connect at node a. 24k and 12.4k connect at node c. 2k, 18k, and 12.4k connect at node e. 10k connects at g.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$, o-]		(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [short]						(5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=1.8~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=24~k$\Omega$, -*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l^=22.7~k$\Omega$, -*]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=12.4~k$\Omega$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, o-]					(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Either a wye-delta transform or a delta-wye transform can be done to reduce the circuit. The following solution utilizes a wye-delta transform. The wye resistances are highlighted in red.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit re-drawn with wye resistances highlighted in red.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit25figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Same circuit as previous with 2k, 22.7k, and 12.4k resistors in red.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$, o-]		(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [short]					(5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=1.8~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=24~k$\Omega$, -*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[red]	(5,5) node[above] {B} to [R, l^=22.7~k$\Omega$, -*, color=red]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[red]	(2.5,2.5) node[left] {A} to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$, color=red]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[red]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=12.4~k$\Omega$, color=red] (5,0) node[below] {C};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, o-]					(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Calculate each of the delta resistances.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_1 \amp= \frac{(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(12.4~\textrm{k}\Omega)+(12.4~\textrm{k}\Omega)(22.7~\textrm{k}\Omega)+(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(22.7~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{12.4~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 28.36~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>R_2 \amp= \frac{(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(12.4~\textrm{k}\Omega)+(12.4~\textrm{k}\Omega)(22.7~\textrm{k}\Omega)+(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(22.7~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{2~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= 175.84~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>R_3	\amp=	\frac{(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(12.4~\textrm{k}\Omega)+(12.4~\textrm{k}\Omega)(22.7~\textrm{k}\Omega)+(2~\textrm{k}\Omega)(22.7~\textrm{k}\Omega)}{22.7~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= 15.49~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Re-draw the circuit.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit re-drawn after the wye-delta transform.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit25figans3">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 10k, 1.8k, 24k, 28.36k, 15.49k, and 175.84k resistors and four nodes. Nodes are labeled same as previous. 10k, 1.8k, 28.36k, and 175.84k connect at node b. 1.8k, 24k, 28.36k, and 15.49k connect at node a. 24k, 15.49k, and 175.84k connect at node c. 10k connects at g.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$, o-]		(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [short]					(5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=1.8~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=24~k$\Omega$, -*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) node[above] {b} to [R, l^=175.84~k$\Omega$, *-*]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) node[left] {a} to [R, l^=28.36~k$\Omega$]		(5,5);	
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=15.49~k$\Omega$]		(5,0) node[below] {c};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, o-]					(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The two resistances (1.8<nbsp />k&#8486; and 28.36<nbsp />k&#8486;) between nodes a and b are in parallel and can be combined, with a parallel resistance of 1.69<nbsp />k&#8486;. The two resistances (24<nbsp />k&#8486; and 15.49<nbsp />k&#8486;) between nodes a and c are in parallel and can be combined, with a parallel resistance of 9.41<nbsp />k&#8486;. The circuit can be re-drawn.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit re-drawn with parallel resistances combined.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit25figans4">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 10k, 1.69k, 9.41k, and 175.84k resistors and four nodes. Nodes are labeled same as previous. 10k, 1.69k, and 175.84k connect at node b. 1.69k and 9.41k connect at node a. 9.41k and 175.84k connect at node c. 10k connects at g.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$, o-]	(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [short]					(5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=1.69~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,2.5) node[left] {a};
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=9.41~k$\Omega$, -*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,5) node[above] {b} to [R, l^=175.84~k$\Omega$] (5,0) node[below] {c};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, o-]					(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 1.69<nbsp />k&#8486; resistance is in series with the 9.41<nbsp />k&#8486; resistance. The equivalent resistance is 11.11<nbsp />k&#8486;. The circuit can be re-drawn.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit re-drawn with series resistances combined.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit25figans5">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 10k, 11.11k and 175.84k resistors and three nodes. Nodes are labeled same as previous. 10k, 11.11k, and 175.84k connect at node b. 11.11k and 175.84k connect at node c. 10k connects at g.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$, o-]		(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [short]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=11.11~k$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) node[above] {b} to [R, l^=175.84~k$\Omega$]		(5,0) node[below] {c};
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, o-]					(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The 11.11<nbsp />k&#8486; and 175.84<nbsp />k&#8486; resistances are in parallel with each other. They have an equivalent resistance of 10.45<nbsp />k&#8486;. The circuit can be re-drawn.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit re-drawn with parallel resistances combined.</caption>
  						<image width="30%" xml:id="ch2-circuit25figans6">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 10k and 10.45k resistors and three nodes. Nodes are labeled same as previous. 10k and 10.45k connect at node b. 10.45k connects at node c. 10k connects at g.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$, o-]		(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10.45~k$\Omega$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, o-]					(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The equivalent resistance is the series combination of the remaining two resistors.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 10~\textrm{k}\Omega+ 10.45~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 20.45~\textrm{k}\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This will not be solved using delta-wye transforms. Do this as a practice exercise. The equivalent resistance calculation should be 20.45<nbsp />k&#8486; regardless of the method used to calculate it. As a hint, the following circuit diagrams show the two possible delta configurations that can be used in a delta-wye transform. The delta resistances are highlighted in red.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit highlighting one possible delta configuration.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-circuit25figansdelta1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 10k, 1.8k (red), 24k, 22.7k (red), 2k (red), and 12.4k resistors and five nodes. Nodes are labeled same as previous. 10k, 1.8k, and 22.7k connect at node b. 1.8k, 2k, and 24k connect at node a. 24k and 12.4k connect at node c. 2k, 18k, and 12.4k connect at node e. 10k connects at g.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$, o-]		(2.5,5);
									\draw[red]	(2.5,5) node[above] {A} to [short]					(5,5);
									\draw[red]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=1.8~k$\Omega$, *-*, color=red]		(2.5,2.5) node[left] {B};
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=24~k$\Omega$, -*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[red]	(5,5) to [R, l^=22.7~k$\Omega$, -*, color=red]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[red]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$, color=red]		(5,2.5) node[right] {C};
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=12.4~k$\Omega$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, o-]					(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit highlighting another possible delta configuration.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-circuit25figansdelta2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 10k, 1.8k, 24k (red), 22.7k, 2k (red), and 12.4k (red) resistors and five nodes. Nodes are labeled same as previous. 10k, 1.8k, and 22.7k connect at node b. 1.8k, 2k, and 24k connect at node a. 24k and 12.4k connect at node c. 2k, 18k, and 12.4k connect at node e. 10k connects at g.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,5) to [R, l^=10~k$\Omega$, o-]		(2.5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [short]					(5,5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,5) to [R, l_=1.8~k$\Omega$, *-]		(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[red]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=24~k$\Omega$, -*, color=red]	(2.5,0) node[below] {B};
									\draw[]	(5,5) to [R, l^=22.7~k$\Omega$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[red]	(2.5,2.5) node[left] {A} to [R, l^=2~k$\Omega$, *-*, color=red]		(5,2.5) node[right] {C};
									\draw[red]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=12.4~k$\Omega$, color=red]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short, o-]					(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Ohm's Law</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch2ohmslawq1">
				<statement>
					<p>If the voltage source can supply a maximum current of 2<nbsp />A, what is the minimum value of <m>R_X</m> that can be used in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit26fig" />. (Assume the power rating of the resistor will be sufficient to handle this load.)</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit26fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2ohmslawq1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch2-circuit26fig">
    						<shortdescription>A circuit with a single mesh containing three circuit elements in series: a 10 V source, a 1.3 ohm resistor, and an unknown resistor labeled "RX."</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=10~V]			(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=1.3~$\Omega$]	(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_X$]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>3.7<nbsp />&#8486;</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate the equivalent resistance required to obtain a current of 2<nbsp />A.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{V}{I}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = \frac{10~\textrm{V}}{2~\textrm{A}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = 5~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Because the two resistors are in series, use the equivalent resistance relationship to calculate the minimum value of <m>R_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 1.3~\Omega + R_X</mrow>
							<mrow>R_X \amp= R_{EQ} - 1.3~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5~\Omega - 1.3~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.7~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>

			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2ohmslawq2">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>I_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit27fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit27fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2ohmslawq2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-circuit27fig">
    						<shortdescription>A circuit with two meshes and three nodes contains a current source "Is," a voltage source "Vs," an 8k resistor, and a 20k resistor. Is and the two resistors connect at node A. The current source flows out of node A. The 20k resistor and high-potential end of Vs connect at node B. The current source, 8k resistor, and low-potential end of the voltage source connect at node C. The current source flows into node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [I, l_=$I_S$]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]								(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=8~k$\Omega$, i_=$I_X$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.75,0) to [open, v_=~~~~~~~~~25~V]			(2.75,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=20~k$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p><minus />3.125<nbsp />mA</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>While this circuit appears complicated, the key is to note that the branch through which <m>I_X</m> is defined contains a known resistance and known voltage drop. This means that all other circuit elements (the current source, voltage source, and 20<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor) are irrelevant to calculating <m>I_X</m>.</p>

					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>I_X</m>. Note that <m>I_X</m> is defined as flowing from low to high potential and therefore must have a negative sign.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_X \amp= -\frac{25~\textrm{V}}{8~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= -3.125~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2ohmslawq3">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the amount of power consumed by the 20<nbsp />&#8486; resistor in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit28fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit28fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2ohmslawq3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-circuit28fig">
    						<shortdescription>An 8mA current source is in parallel with a 20ohm resistor and a 5ohm resistor.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=8~mA]					(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=5~$\Omega$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>51.2<nbsp />&#181;W</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Because this question emphasizes the use of Ohm's law, this solution will emphasize the use of Ohm's law and the power equation. (An alternative solution method is presented at the end of this solution.)</p>

					<p>First, calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit (this will allow us to determine the voltage dropped over each resistor).</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= \frac{(20~\Omega)(5~\Omega)}{20~\Omega+5~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now, use Ohm's law to calculate the voltage dropped over each resistor. (Note the unit of current has been converted to A.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V \amp= (0.008~\textrm{A})(4~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow> \amp= 0.032~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>At this point, it's possible to use <xref ref="ohmspower3" /> to calculate the power consumed by the 20<nbsp />&#8486; resistor directly. It's also possible to use Ohm's law a second time to calculate the current through the 20<nbsp />&#8486; resistor and then use <xref ref="ohmspower1" />.</p>

					<p>Calculating directly using <xref ref="ohmspower3" />, we calculate that the power consumed by the resistor is 51.2<nbsp />&#181;W.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>P \amp= \frac{V^2}{R}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{(0.032~\textrm{V})^2}{20~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{0.001024~\textrm{V}^2}{20~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.12\times10^{-5}~\textrm{W}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 51.2~\mu\textrm{W}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Using the alternative method presented above, use Ohm's law to calculate the current through the 20<nbsp />&#8486; resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I \amp= \frac{V}{R}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = \frac{0.032~\textrm{V}}{20~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = 0.0016~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Then use <xref ref="ohmspower1" /> to calculate the power.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>P \amp= IV</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (0.0016~\textrm{A})(0.032~\textrm{V})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5.12\times10^{-5}~\textrm{W}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 51.2~\mu\textrm{W}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Another method would be to use the current divider rule to calculate the current through the 20<nbsp />&#8486; resistor and then use <xref ref="ohmspower2" /> to calculate the power it consumes. We have already previously calculated the equivalent resistance of the circuit.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I \amp= (8~\textrm{mA})\left(\frac{R_{EQ}}{20~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (8~\textrm{mA})\left(\frac{4~\Omega}{20~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = 1.6~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now use <xref ref="ohmspower2" /> to calculate the power consumed by the resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>P \amp= I^2R</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = (1.6 \times 10^{-3}~\textrm{A})^2(20~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = (2.56 \times 10^{-6}~\textrm{A}^2)(20~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = 5.12 \times 10^{-5}~\textrm{W}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = 51.2~\mu\textrm{W}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>All three solution methods yielded the same result, as expected.</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2ohmslawq4">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the amount of power supplied by the source in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit29fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit29fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2ohmslawq4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="50%" xml:id="ch2-circuit29fig">
    						<shortdescription>A circuit with two meshes and three nodes, containing a 5A source, 10ohm resistor, 50ohm resistor, and 100ohm resistor. The 5A source, 10ohm resistor, and 50ohm resistor connect at node A. The current source flows into node A. The 50ohm and 100ohm resistors connect at node B. The 5A source, 10ohm resistor, and 100ohm resistor connect at node C. The current source flows out of node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=5~A]					(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=50~$\Omega$]		(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</statement>

				<hint>
					<p>Use conservation of power.</p>
				</hint>

				<answer>
					<p>234<nbsp />W</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The circuit can be reduced to an equivalent circuit containing one 5<nbsp />A source and one resistor. Calculate the equivalent resistance as seen by the source to calculate this resistance value. (The 50<nbsp />&#8486; and 100<nbsp />&#8486; resistors are in series. This series resistance is in parallel with the 10<nbsp />&#8486; resistor.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= (50~\Omega + 100~\Omega) // (10~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 150~\Omega // 10~\Omega</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp = 9.375~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Conservation of power states that the power supplied by the source must be equal to the power consumed by this equivalent resistance. Use <xref ref="ohmspower2" /> to calculate the power consumed by <m>R_{EQ}</m> (which is equal to the power supplied by the source).</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>P \amp= I^2R</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (5~\textrm{A})^2(9.375~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= (25~\textrm{A}^2)(9.375~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 234~\textrm{W}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Alternatively, a combination of Ohm's law and power calculations (or current divider and power calculations) can be used to calculate the power consumed by each individual resistor (10<nbsp />&#8486;, 50<nbsp />&#8486;, and 100<nbsp />&#8486;) and those power values can be summed to calculate the total power consumption, which is equal to the power supplied by the source. This process will not be shown in this book but can be used for extra practice. This calculation will also yield 234<nbsp />W.</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2ohmslawq5">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate the minimum value of <m>R</m> that can be used to keep the power consumed by either resistor to less than or equal to 250<nbsp />mW in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit210fig" />. Both resistors in <xref ref="circuit210fig" /> have the same value of <m>R</m>.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit210fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2ohmslawq5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch2-circuit210fig">
    						<shortdescription>A 20V source in series with two resistors, each labeled "R."</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]		(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R$]		(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]			(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>400<nbsp />&#8486;</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>First, use the voltage divider rule to calculate the voltage that will drop over each individual resistor. (It's possible to skip this step if you note that, because the two resistor values are identical, they will both drop voltages equal to one-half of the source voltage.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{R} \amp= 20~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{R}{R+R}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 20~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{R}{2R}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 20~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 10~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Use <xref ref="ohmspower3" /> to calculate <m>R_{min}</m>, given that <m>P_{max}</m> is 0.25<nbsp />W.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>R_{min} \amp= \frac{V^2}{P_{max}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{(10~\textrm{V})^2}{0.25~\textrm{W}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{(100~\textrm{V}^2)}{0.25~\textrm{W}}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 400~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Voltage and Current Divider</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch2dividerq1">
				<statement>
					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>V_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit211fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit211fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2dividerq1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch2-circuit211fig">
    						<shortdescription>A 7.2V source is in series with a 3k resistor and a 6k resistor. The 6k resistor drops voltage Vx. The high-potential end of Vx is oriented toward the high-potential end of the voltage source.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=7.2~V]					(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=3~k$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=6~k$\Omega$, v^=$V_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>4.8<nbsp />V</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Use the voltage divider rule.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= 7.2~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{6~\textrm{k}\Omega}{3~\textrm{k}\Omega+6~\textrm{k}\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 7.2~\textrm{V}(0.67)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 4.8~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2dividerq2">
				<statement>
					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>I_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit212fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit212fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2dividerq2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-circuit212fig">
    						<shortdescription>A 500mA source is in parallel with a 42ohm resistor, a 12ohm resistor, and a 24ohm resistor. The current through the 24ohm resistor is labeled "Ix." Ix flows into the same node that the current source flows out of.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=500~mA]						(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=42~$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=12~$\Omega$, *-*]			(5,0);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=24~$\Omega$, i^=$I_X$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>140<nbsp />mA</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>First calculate the equivalent resistance of the circuit. All three resistors are connected in parallel.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>\frac{1}{R_{EQ}} \amp= \frac{1}{42~\Omega} + \frac{1}{12~\Omega} + \frac{1}{24~\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.0238~\Omega^{-1} + 0.083~\Omega^{-1} + 0.042~\Omega^{-1}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.149~\Omega^{-1}</mrow>
							<mrow>R_{EQ} \amp= 6.72~\Omega</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now use the current divider rule to calculate <m>I_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_X \amp= 500~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{6.72~\Omega}{24~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 140~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2dividerq3">
				<statement>
					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>V_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit213fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit213fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2dividerq3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-circuit213fig">
    						<shortdescription>A circuit with two nodes and four meshes containing an 8V source, 220ohm resistor, 330ohm resistor, 100ohm resistor, and 470ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the voltage source and the 220ohm resistor connect at node A. The 220ohm, 330ohm, and 100ohm resistors connect at node B. The 100ohm and 470ohm resistors connect at node C. The low-potential end of the voltage source, 330ohm resistor, and 470ohm resistor connect at node D. Voltage drop Vx is defined over the 470ohm resistor with the drop defined from node C to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=8~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=220~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=330~$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$]			(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=470~$\Omega$, v^=$V_X$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<hint>
					<p>
						Read through <xref ref="ch2-voltagedivider-twomesh-example" /> and check for similarities between the two circuits.
					</p>
				</hint>

				<answer>
					<p>3.21<nbsp />V</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The voltage divider rule cannot be applied directly to this circuit yet as not all resistances are in series with the voltage source. An equivalent circuit to this was solved in <xref ref="ch2-voltagedivider-twomesh-example" />. Use that analysis method to calculate <m>V_X</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= 8~\textrm{V}\left(\frac{470~\Omega}{100~\Omega+470~\Omega}\right)\left(\frac{330~\Omega//(100~\Omega+470~\Omega)}{220~\Omega+(330~\Omega//(100~\Omega+470~\Omega))}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8~\textrm{V}(0.8246)\left(\frac{330~\Omega//(570~\Omega)}{220~\Omega+(330~\Omega//570~\Omega)}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8~\textrm{V}(0.8246)\left(\frac{209~\Omega}{220~\Omega+209~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8~\textrm{V}(0.8246)(0.4872)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3.21~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2dividerq4">
				<statement>
					<p>Use the current divider rule to calculate <m>I_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit214" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit214">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2dividerq4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-circuit214">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 3 nodes containing a 210mA source, 400ohm resistor, 25ohm resistor, 200ohm resistor, and 150ohm resistor. The current source, 400ohm resistor, and 25ohm resistor connect at node A. The current source flows into node A. The 25ohm, 200ohm, and 150ohm resistors connect at node B. The current source, 400ohm, 200ohm, and 150ohm resistors connect at node C. The current source flows out of node C. Current "Ix" is defined through the 150ohm resistor and flows out of node B into node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=210~mA]						(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]							(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=400~$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=25~$\Omega$]			(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=200~$\Omega$, *-*]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=150~$\Omega$, i^=$I_X$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<hint>
					<p>
						Read through <xref ref="ch2-currentdivider-threemesh-example" /> and check for similarities between the two circuits.
					</p>
				</hint>

				<answer>
					<p>94.0<nbsp />mA</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The current divider rule cannot be applied directly to this circuit yet as not all resistances are in parallel with the current source. An equivalent circuit to this was solved in <xref ref="ch2-currentdivider-threemesh-example" />. Use that analysis method to calculate <m>I_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_X \amp= 210~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{400~\Omega // (25~\Omega + (200~\Omega // 150~\Omega))}{25~\Omega + (200~\Omega // 150~\Omega)}\right)\left(\frac{200~\Omega // 150~\Omega}{150~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 210~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{400~\Omega // (25~\Omega + 85.7143)}{25~\Omega + 85.7143}\right)\left(\frac{85.7143}{150~\Omega}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 210~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{400~\Omega // 110.7143}{110.7143}\right)(0.5714)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 210~\textrm{mA}\left(\frac{86.7133}{110.7143}\right)(0.5714)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 210~\textrm{mA}(0.7832)(0.5714)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 94.0~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2dividerq5">
				<statement>
					<p>Use the voltage divider rule to calculate <m>V_{X1}</m> and <m>V_{X2}</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit215" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit215">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2dividerq5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-circuit215">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 6 nodes containing source Vs, and resistors R1, R2, R3, R4, R5, R6, and R7. R2 and R3 connect at node A. R1 and R2 connect at node B. R1, the high-potential end of Vs, and R4 connect at node C. R4, R5, and R6 connect at node D. R6 and R7 connect at node E. R3, the low-potential end of Vs, R5, and R7 connect at node F. Vx1 is defined over R3 and drops from node A to node F. Vx2 is defined over R7 and drops from node E to node F.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$, *-*]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_4$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_5$, *-*]				(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_6$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_7$, v^=$V_{X2}$]		(5,0);
									\draw[]	(-5,0) to [short]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]					(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(-5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]					(-2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(-5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_3$, v^=$V_{X1}$]		(-5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{X1} \amp= V_S\left(\frac{R_3}{R_1+R_2+R_3}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>V_{X2} \amp= V_S\left[\frac{R_7}{R_6+R_7}\right]\left[\frac{R_5//(R_6+R_7)}{R_4+R_5//(R_6+R_7)}\right]</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>The two <q>halves</q> of the circuit can be treated separately from each other. This is because the two halves are in parallel, and, from a standpoint of voltage, have identical voltage drops. First, let's consider the left <q>half,</q> re-drawn below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Left <q>half</q> of the circuit.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit215ans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit half has one mesh and 4 nodes, and contains only R1, R2, R3, and Vs.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]					(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(-5,0);
									\draw[]	(-2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_1$]					(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(-5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_2$]					(-2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(-5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_3$, v^=$V_{X1}$]		(-5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>All of the resistors are in series with the voltage source, so the voltage divider rule can be applied directly to calculate <m>V_{X1}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>V_{X1} = V_S\left(\frac{R_3}{R_1+R_2+R_3}\right)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now, let's consider the right <q>half,</q>, re-drawn below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Right <q>half</q> of the circuit.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit215ans2">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit half has two meshes and 4 nodes, and contains only Vs, R4, R5, R6, and R7.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=$V_S$]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_4$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_5$, *-*]			(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=$R_6$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l_=$R_7$, v^=$V_{X2}$]	(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>To calculate <m>V_{X2}</m>, the process used in <xref ref="ch2dividerq3" /> will be used again. Be sure you are comfortable with that process before proceeding, as not every step will be exhaustively recounted in this solution.</p>

					<p>The voltage drop over resistor <m>R_5</m> can be used to calculate <m>V_{X2}</m>. To calculate that voltage drop, we must first calculate the equivalent resistance seen by <m>R_5</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>R_{EQ,R5} = R_5//(R_6+R_7)</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now, use the voltage divider rule to calculate the voltage drop over this equivalent resistance.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{R5} \amp= V_S\left(\frac{R_{EQ,R5}}{R_4+R_{EQ,R5}}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= V_S\left(\frac{R_5//(R_6+R_7)}{R_4+R_5//(R_6+R_7)}\right)</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now, use the voltage divider rule with <m>V_{R5}</m> as the source voltage to calculate <m>V_{X2}</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_{X2} \amp= V_{R5}\left(\frac{R_7}{R_6+R_7}\right)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= V_S\left[\frac{R_7}{R_6+R_7}\right]\left[\frac{R_5//(R_6+R_7)}{R_4+R_5//(R_6+R_7)}\right]</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				
				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Kirchhoff's Laws</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch2kirchhoffq1">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>V_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit216" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit216">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2kirchhoffq1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="40%" xml:id="ch2-circuit216">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit contains a 5V source and two unknown circuit elements. One unknown circuit element drops 2V (same polarity as source) and the other drops Vx, defined with the same polarity as the source.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V]				(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic, v^=2~V]		(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, v^=$V_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]					(2.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>3<nbsp />V</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Apply KVL around the loop. This calculation will start in the lower left corner of the circuit and move around clockwise.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= -5~\textrm{V} + 2~\textrm{V} + V_X</mrow>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= 5~\textrm{V} - 2~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 3~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2kirchhoffq2">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>I_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit217fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit217fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2kirchhoffq2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit217fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit has 2 meshes and 3 nodes and contains a 10mA source and three unknown elements (E1, E2, and E3). The source, E1, and E2 connect at node A. The source flows into node A. E2 and E3 connect at node B. The source, E1, and E3 connect at node C. The source flows out of node C. A 4mA current is defined as flowing through E1 out of node A into node C. Unknown current Ix is defined as flowing through E2 and E3 out of node B into node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=10~mA]					(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, i^=4~mA, *-*]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [generic, i^=$I_X$]			(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>6<nbsp />mA</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Apply KCL at one of the nodes. The top node will be used in this calculation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= 10~\textrm{mA} - 4~\textrm{mA} - I_X</mrow>
							<mrow>I_X \amp= 10~\textrm{mA} - 4~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2kirchhoffq3">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>I_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit218fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit218fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2kirchhoffq3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit218fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with two meshes and four nodes containing a 5V source, 3V source, 1k resistor, 100ohm resistor, and another 1k resistor. The high-potential end of 5V and a 1k resistor connect at node A. All three resistors connect at node B. The other 1k resistor and the high-potential end of the 3V source connect at node C. The low-potential end of both sources and the 100ohm resistor connect at node D. Current Ix is defined through the 100ohm resistor flowing out of node B into node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]						(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$I_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=3~V]								(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>6.67<nbsp />mA</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>There are two approaches to solve this problem. The first is to define a voltage drop over the 100<nbsp />&#8486; resistor and then use KCL at the node connecting the 1<nbsp />k&#8486;, 100<nbsp />&#8486;, and 1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors. The second is to use a set of matrix equations to solve for <m>I_X</m>.</p>
					
					<p>Let's start with the first approach. The circuit has been re-drawn to define the unknown voltage (let's call it <m>V_X</m>) and label the node of interest.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit re-drawn to define <m>V_X</m> and label the node of interest for performing KCL.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit218figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Same circuit as above, with Vx defined as dropping over the 100ohm resistor from node B to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5) node[above] {A};
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, *-*, i_=$I_X$, v^=$V_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=3~V]							(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The branch currents through the two 1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistors can be defined as flowing <em>into</em> node A. (This is an arbitrary choice.) The current through the left 1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor (let's call it <m>I_{L}</m>) can be calculated in terms of <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>I_L = \frac{5~\textrm{V} - V_X}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>The current through the right 1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor (let's call it <m>I_{R}</m>) can also be calculated in terms of <m>V_X</m>.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>I_R = \frac{3~\textrm{V} - V_X}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega}</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Branch current <m>I_X</m> is defined as flowing <em>out of</em> node A. This current can be calculated in terms of <m>V_X</m>. (Note the unit of the 100<nbsp />&#8486; resistor has been converted to k&#8486; to be consistent with the other branch current calculations.)</p>

					<p>
						<md>I_X = \frac{V_X}{0.1~\textrm{k}\Omega}</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use KCL between <m>I_L</m>, <m>I_R</m>, and <m>I_X</m> at node A. This allows us to calculate <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0 \amp= I_L + I_R - I_X</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= \frac{5~\textrm{V} - V_X}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega} + \frac{3~\textrm{V} - V_X}{1~\textrm{k}\Omega} - \frac{V_X}{0.1~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5~\textrm{V} - V_X + 3~\textrm{V} - V_X - \frac{V_X}{0.1}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 5~\textrm{V} - V_X + 3~\textrm{V} - V_X - 10V_X</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 8~\textrm{V} - 12V_X</mrow>
							<mrow>12V_X \amp= 8~\textrm{V}</mrow>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= 0.667~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>I_X</m>. (Note that the unit of current will me mA due to the use of V and k&#8486;.)</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_X \amp= \frac{0.667~\textrm{V}}{0.1~\textrm{k}\Omega}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 6.67~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Now let's use the second approach to solve for <m>I_X</m>. We will explicitly define branch currents to use in our calculations. The circuit is re-drawn with these branch currents defined below. (The direction of each branch current is chosen arbitrarily.)</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit re-drawn to define unknown branch currents.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit218figans2">
    						<shortdescription>Same circuit as above with I1 defined through the 1k resistor flowing out of node A into node B. I2 flows through the other 1k resistor flowing out of node B into node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=5~V]								(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_1$]			(2.5,2.5) node[above] {A};
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=100~$\Omega$, *-*, i_=$I_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=1~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_2$]			(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l_=3~V]								(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]									(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>As there are three unknowns (<m>I_1</m>, <m>I_2</m>, and <m>I_X</m>), we will need to derive three equations. All units are in V, mA, and k&#8486; and will be omitted from the KCL and KVL equations to make them easier to read.</p>

					<p>Node A is a perfect node. Using KCL at this node we obtain one equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>I_1 - I_2 - I_X = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>The left mesh is a perfect loop. Using KVL around this loop (starting in the lower left and moving clockwise), we obtain a second equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>-5 + 1I_1 + 0.1I_X = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>The right mesh is a perfect loop. Using KVL around this loop (starting in the lower left and moving clockwise), we obtain the third equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>-0.1I_X + 1I_2 + 3 = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>I_1</m>, the middle-left column contains coefficients of <m>I_2</m>, the middle-right column contains coefficients of <m>I_X</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp -1 	\amp -1 		\amp 0\\
								1 	\amp 0 		\amp 0.1		\amp 5\\
								0	\amp 1		\amp -0.1		\amp -3
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0 		\amp 4.33\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0		\amp -2.33\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 1		\amp 6.67
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Read the last row to determine that <m>I_X = 6.67~\textrm{mA}</m>.</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2kirchhoffq4">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>V_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit219fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit219fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2kirchhoffq4" />.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-circuit219fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 5 nodes containing a 10V source, 2V source, 100ohm resistor, 50ohm resistor, 200ohm resistor, and 1k resistor. The high-potential end of the 10V source and 100ohm resistor connect at node A. The 100ohm resistor, 50ohm resistor, and high-potential end of the 2V source connect at node B. The low-potential end of the 2V source and the 200ohm resistor connect at node C. The 200ohm and 1k resistors connect at node D. The low-potential end of the 10V source, the 50ohm resistor, and the 1k resistor connect at node E. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 1k resistor from node D to node E.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=10~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [V, l^=2~V]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]				(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, v^=$V_X$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>1.08<nbsp />V</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>KCL and KVL equations will be used to derive branch currents. Then, Ohm's law will be used to relate the branch current through the 1<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor to calculate <m>V_X</m>. There are three branches, so the circuit will be re-drawn to define each branch current (the directions have been selected arbitrarily). The perfect node in the circuit is also labeled on the circuit diagram.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with all branch currents defined and the perfect node labeled.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-circuit219figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Same circuit as above. I1 is defined through the 100ohm resistor flowing out of node A and into node B. I2 is defined through the 50ohm resistor flowing out of node B and into node E. I3 is defined through the 200ohm resistor flowing out of node C into node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=10~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$, i^=$I_1$]		(2.5,2.5) node[above] {a};
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=50~$\Omega$, *-*, i^=$I_2$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [V, l^=2~V]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$, i^=$I_3$]		(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, v^=$V_X$]		(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>As there are three unknowns (<m>I_1</m>, <m>I_2</m>, and <m>I_3</m>), we will need to derive three equations. All units are in V, mA, and k&#8486; and will be omitted from the KCL and KVL equations to make them easier to read.</p>

					<p>Node a is a perfect node. Using KCL at this node we obtain one equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>I_1 - I_2 - I_3 = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>The left mesh is a perfect loop. Using KVL around this loop (starting in the lower left and moving clockwise), we obtain a second equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>-10 + 0.1I_1 + 0.05I_2 = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>The right mesh is a perfect loop. Using KVL around this loop (starting in the lower left and moving clockwise), we obtain the third equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>-0.05I_2 + 2 + 0.2I_3 + 1I_3 = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>I_1</m>, the middle-left column contains coefficients of <m>I_2</m>, the middle-right column contains coefficients of <m>I_3</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 		\amp -1 		\amp -1 		\amp 0\\
								0.1 	\amp 0.05		\amp 0			\amp 10\\
								0		\amp -0.05		\amp 1.2		\amp -2
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0 		\amp 67.03\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0		\amp 65.95\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 1		\amp 1.08
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Read the last row to determine that <m>I_3 = 1.08~\textrm{mA}</m>. Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= (1.08~\textrm{mA})(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.08~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2kirchhoffq5">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate <m>V_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit220fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit220fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2kirchhoffq5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-circuit220fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 3 nodes containing CCCS of 0.2*IX, 320V source, 48k resistor, and 100k resistor. The CCCS, 48k, and 100k resistors connect at node A. The CCCS flows into node A. The 100k resistor and high-potential end of the 320V source connect at node B. The CCCS, 48k resistor, and low-potential end of the 320V source connect at node C. The CCCS flows out of node D. Ix is defined through the 48k resistor and flows out of node A into node C. Vx drops over the 48k resistor and drops from node A to node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [cI, l^=0.2$I_X$]									(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=48~k$\Omega$, *-*, i_=$I_X$, v^=$V_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=100~k$\Omega$]							(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l^=320~V]									(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>120<nbsp />V</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>As drawn, the circuit contains zero perfect nodes and zero perfect loops. To remedy this, the branch current through the 100<nbsp />k&#8486; resistor will be defined. The re-drawn circuit is shown below.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with all branch currents defined and the perfect node labeled.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit220figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Same circuit as above with I1 defined through the 100k resistor flowing out of node B into node C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [cI, l^=0.2$I_X$]									(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [short]											(2.5,2.5) node[above] {A};
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=48~k$\Omega$, *-*, i_=$I_X$, v^=$V_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=100~k$\Omega$, i^=$I_1$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [V, l^=320~V]									(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]											(5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>The circuit now has one perfect node and one perfect loop. There are two unknowns, so two equations will be derived. All units are in V, mA, and k&#8486; and will be omitted from the KCL and KVL equations to make them easier to read.</p>

					<p>Node a is a perfect node. Using KCL at this node we obtain one equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>0.2I_X - I_X - I_1 = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>The right mesh is a perfect loop. Using KVL around this loop (starting in the lower left and moving clockwise), we obtain the second equation.</p>

					<p>
						<md>-48I_X + 100I_1 + 320 = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>I_X</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>I_1</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								-0.8 	\amp -1 	\amp 0\\
								-48		\amp 100	\amp -320
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 2.5\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp -2\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Read the first row to determine that <m>I_X = 2.5~\textrm{mA}</m>. Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= (2.5~\textrm{mA})(48~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 120~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>This circuit can also be solved in a similar manner to <xref ref="ch2kirchhoffq3" />. However, it may not be as intuitive. The equations below use units of V, k&#8486;, and mA and will not be explicitly shown. We know that</p>

					<p>
						<md>0.2I_X = I_X + I_1</md>.
					</p>

					<p>In addition, we know that</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_X \amp=\frac{V_X}{48}</mrow>
							<mrow>I_1 \amp= \frac{V_X - 320}{100}</mrow>
						</md>.
					</p>

					<p>Use these three equations to solve for <m>V_X</m> directly.</p>
					
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>0.2\left(\frac{V_X}{48}\right) \amp= \frac{V_X}{48} + \frac{V_X - 320}{100}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{V_X}{240} \amp= \frac{V_X}{48} + \frac{V_X - 320}{100}</mrow>
							<mrow>\frac{5V_X}{1200} \amp= \frac{25V_X}{1200} + \frac{12V_X - 3840}{1200}</mrow>
							<mrow>5V_X \amp= 25V_X + 12V_X - 3840</mrow>
							<mrow>-32V_X \amp= -3840</mrow>
							<mrow>V_X = 120~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

		<subexercises>
			<title>Mesh Analysis</title>
		
			<exercise xml:id="ch2meshq1">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate mesh currents <m>I_A</m> and <m>I_B</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit221fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit221fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2meshq1" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit221fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 4 nodes containing a 10mA source and four unknown circuit elements (E1, E2, E3, and E4). The 10mA source and E1 connect at node A. The source flows into node A. E1, E2, and E3 connect at node B. E3 and E4 connect at node C. The source, E2, and E4 connect at node D. The source flows out of node D. Mesh current A is defined clockwise and flows through nodes A, B, and D in that order. Mesh current B is defined clockwise and flows through nodes B, C, and D in that order. A branch current of 2mA is defined as flowing out of node B into node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [I, l^=10~mA]					(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, *-*, i_=2~mA]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [generic]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
									\draw[Stealth-] (1.25,1.25)node{$I_A$}  		++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (3.75,1.25)node{$I_B$}  		++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_A \amp= 10~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>I_B \amp= 8~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Because mesh <m>I_A</m> contains a current source, <m>I_A</m> is equal to that same current. They share the same sign because the currents point in the same direction. Therefore, <m>I_A = 10~\textrm{mA}</m>.</p>

					<p>Use the relationship between the 2<nbsp />mA branch current and mesh currents <m>I_A</m> and <m>I_B</m> to calculate <m>I_B</m>. (The 2<nbsp />mA branch current is used because it is known and is also shared by both mesh currents.) In this calculation, <m>I_A</m> is in the same direction as the 2<nbsp />mA branch current and <m>I_B</m> is in the opposite direction as the 2<nbsp />mA branch current.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_A - I_B \amp= 2~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>10~\textrm{mA} - I_B \amp= 2~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>-I_B \amp= -8~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
							<mrow>I_B \amp= 8~\textrm{mA}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2meshq2">
				<statement>
					<p>A branch is shared by two clockwise meshes. The left mesh current is 3<nbsp />A and the right mesh current is <minus />6<nbsp />A. Calculate the branch current. Assume the branch current is in the same direction as the left mesh current and in the opposite direction as the right mesh current.</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>9<nbsp />A</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Draw the circuit to define the mesh and branch currents. The branch current is labeled <m>I_X</m>.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2meshq2" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit222fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes, 4 nodes, and 5 unknown circuit elements (E1-E5). E1 and E2 connect at node A. E2, E3, and E4 connect at node B. E4 and E5 connect at node C. E1, E3, and E5 connect at node D. A mesh current of 3A flows through E1, E2, and E3 through nodes D, A, B, and D. A mesh current of -6A flows through E3, E4, and E5 through nodes D, B, C, and D. Branch current Ix is defined as flowing through E3 from node B to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [generic]					(0,2.5);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [generic]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic, *-*, i_=$I_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [generic]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [generic]					(5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]						(5,0);
									\draw[Stealth-] (1.25,1.25)node{3~A}  			++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (3.75,1.25)node{--6~A}  		++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Use the relationship between branch and mesh currents to calculate the branch current <m>I_X</m>. The 3<nbsp />A mesh current is in the same direction as <m>I_X</m> and the <minus />6<nbsp />A mesh current is in the opposite direction as <m>I_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_X \amp= 3~\textrm{A} - (-6~\textrm{A})</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 9~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2meshq3">
				<statement>
					<p>Calculate mesh current <m>I_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit223fig" />. Assume that each mesh contains at least one linear circuit element.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit223fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2meshq3" />.</caption>
  						<image width="60%" xml:id="ch2-circuit223fig">
    						<shortdescription>Nine meshes are arranged in a 3x3 pattern. Every mesh current is clockwise. From top to bottom, left to right, the mesh currents are labeled I1, I2, I3, etc. through to I8. The central mesh is labeled "Ix."</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\foreach \x in {0, 1.5} {
										\foreach \y in {0, 1.5, 3, 4.5} {
											\draw[]	(\x, \y) to [short, -*] ++ (1.5,0);
										}
									}
									\foreach \y in {0, 1.5, 3, 4.5} {
										\draw[]	(3, \y) to [short] ++ (1.5,0);
									}
									\foreach \x in {0, 1.5, 3, 4.5} {
										\draw[]	(\x, 0) to [short] ++ (0,4.5);
									}
									\foreach \x/\y/\n in {0.75/3.75/1, 2.25/3.75/2, 3.75/3.75/3, 0.75/2.25/4, 2.25/2.25/X, 3.75/2.25/5, 0.75/0.75/6, 2.25/0.75/7, 3.75/0.75/8} {
										\draw[Stealth-] (\x, \y) node{$I_\n$}  ++(-60:0.4) arc (-60:170:0.4);
									}
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						<md>I_X = -I_2 - I_4 - I_5 - I_7</md>
					</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Note that all of the numbered mesh currents are also branch currents. The only mesh current that isn't a branch current is in the center (<m>I_X</m>). Use the relationship between branch and mesh currents to calculate <m>I_X</m>. Note that <m>I_X</m> is defined in an opposing direction to all adjacent mesh currents, which is why the signs of each numbered mesh current is negative.</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2meshq4">
				<statement>
					<p>Use mesh analysis to calculate <m>V_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit219fig" /> (in the Kirchhoff's laws section).</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>1.08<nbsp />V</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Draw the circuit with each mesh current defined. Because there are no current sources, there are no relationships between sources and mesh currents either directly or via a supermesh.</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit diagram with all mesh currents defined.</caption>
  						<image width="100%" xml:id="ch2-circuit224figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 2 meshes and 5 nodes containing a 10V source, 100ohm resistor, 50ohm resistor, 2V source, 200ohm resistor, and 1k resistor. The high-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 100ohm resistor at node A. The 100ohm resistor, 50ohm resistor, and high-potential end of the 2V source connect at node B. The low-potential end of the 2V source connects to the 200ohm resistor at node C. The 200ohm and 1k resistors connect at node D. The low-potential end of the 10V source connects to the 50ohm and 1k resistors at node E. Voltage Vx is defined as dropping over the 1k resistor from node D to node E. Mesh current Ia flows through the 10V source, 100ohm resistor, and 50ohm resistor through nodes E, A, B, and E. Mesh current Ib flows through the 50ohm resistor, 2V source, 200ohm resistor, and 1k resistor through nodes E, B, C, D, and E.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=10~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=100~$\Omega$]				(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=50~$\Omega$, *-*]		(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [V, l^=2~V]					(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [R, l^=200~$\Omega$]				(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=1~k$\Omega$, v^=$V_X$]	(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]							(7.5,0);
									\draw[Stealth-] (1.25,1.25)node{$I_A$}  			++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (5,1.25)node{$I_B$}  				++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>Derive both mesh equations, starting in the lower left of each mesh and moving clockwise. All units are in V, mA, and k&#8486; and will be omitted from the mesh equations to make them easier to read.</p>

					<p>The left mesh equation is:</p>

					<p>
						<md>-10 +0.1I_A + 0.05 (I_A-I_B) = 0.</md>
					</p>

					<p>The right mesh equation is:</p>

					<p>
						<md>0.05(I_B-I_A) + 2 + 0.2I_B + 1I_B = 0.</md>
					</p>

					<p>The two equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>I_A</m>, the middle column contains coefficients of <m>I_B</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								0.15 	\amp -0.05 	\amp 10\\
								-0.05	\amp 1.25	\amp -2
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 67.03\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 1.08\\
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Read the second row to determine that <m>I_B = 1.08~\textrm{mA}</m>. Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= (1.08~\textrm{mA})(1~\textrm{k}\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 1.08~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Note that this is (and should be) the same answer as <xref ref="ch2kirchhoffq4" />.</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>

			<exercise xml:id="ch2meshq5">
				<statement>
					<p>Use mesh analysis to calculate <m>V_X</m> in the circuit shown in <xref ref="circuit225fig" />.</p>

					<figure xml:id="circuit225fig">
  						<caption>Circuit diagram corresponding to <xref ref="ch2meshq5" />.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-circuit225fig">
    						<shortdescription>Circuit with 3 meshes and 4 nodes containing a 20V source, 8mA source, 10ohm resistor, 20ohm resistor, 60ohm resistor, and 10ohm resistor. The high-potential end of the 20V source and a 10ohm resistor connect at node A. A 10ohm resistor, 20ohm resistor, and 60ohm resistor connect at node B. The 60ohm resistor, 8mA source, and the other 10ohm resistor connect at node C. The 8mA source flows into node C. The low-potential end of the 20V source, the 20ohm resistor, the 8mA source, and the other 10ohm resistor connect at node D. The 8mA source flows out of node D. Vx is defined over the 20ohm resistor and drops from node B to node D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]							(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l_=20~$\Omega$, *-*, v^=$V_X$]	(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=60~$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, l^=8~mA, *-*]						(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]								(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l_=10~$\Omega$]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>12.2<nbsp />V</p>
				</answer>

				<solution>
					<p>Redraw the circuit to define the mesh currents. (Note that some parameter values have been removed to make it easier to read the mesh current labels.)</p>

					<figure>
  						<caption>Circuit re-drawn to display mesh currents.</caption>
  						<image width="80%" xml:id="ch2-circuit225figans1">
    						<shortdescription>Same circuit as above. Mesh current Ia flows through the 20V source, 10ohm resistor, and 20ohm resistor through nodes D, A, B, and D. Mesh current Ib flows through the 20ohm resistor, 60ohm resistor, and 8mA source through nodes D, , C, and D. Mesh current Ic flows through the 8mA source and 10ohm resistor through nodes D, C, and D.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\sffamily]
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [V, l_=20~V]						(0,0);
									\draw[]	(0,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]					(2.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, *-*, v^=$V_X$]					(2.5,0);
									\draw[]	(2.5,2.5) to [R, l^=60~$\Omega$]				(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,0) to [I, *-*]								(5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(5,2.5) to [short]							(7.5,2.5);
									\draw[]	(7.5,2.5) to [R, l^=10~$\Omega$]				(7.5,0);
									\draw[]	(0,0) to [short]								(7.5,0);
									\draw[Stealth-] (1.25,1.25)node{$I_A$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (3.75,1.25)node{$I_B$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
									\draw[Stealth-] (6.25,1.25)node{$I_C$}  ++(-60:0.5) arc (-60:170:0.5);
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
  						</image>
					</figure>

					<p>All subsequent units are in V, A, and &#8486; and will be omitted from the mesh equations to make them easier to read.</p>

					<p>The left mesh does not contain any current sources. The KCL equation corresponding to the left mesh is:</p>

					<p>
						<md>-20 + 10I_A + 20(I_A-I_B) = 0</md>
					</p>

					<p>The middle and right meshes share a current source. This is a supermesh. KVL will be done around the supermesh, and the relationship between the branch and mesh currents will be used to derive the two equations for these two meshes.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>20(I_B-I_A) + 60I_B + 10I_C \amp= 0</mrow>
							<mrow>I_C - I_B \amp= 0.008</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The three equations can be expressed in a matrix. The left column contains coefficients of <m>I_A</m>, the middle-left column contains coefficients of <m>I_B</m>, the middle-right column contains coefficients of <m>I_C</m>, and the right column contains constants.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								30 	\amp -20 	\amp 0 		\amp 20\\
								-20	\amp 80		\amp 10		\amp 0\\
								0	\amp 1		\amp -1		\amp -0.008
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>The matrix is shown in reduced row echelon form below.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							\begin{bmatrix} 
								1 	\amp 0 	\amp 0	\amp 0.782\\
								0 	\amp 1 	\amp 0	\amp 0.173\\
								0	\amp 0	\amp 1	\amp 0.181
							\end{bmatrix}
						</md>
					</p>

					<p>Read the top row to determine that <m>I_A = 0.782~\textrm{A}</m>, and read the second row to determine that <m>I_B = 0.173~\textrm{A}</m>. Use the relationship between mesh and branch currents to calculate the branch current through the 20<nbsp />&#8486; resistor.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>I_{20} \amp= I_A - I_B</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.782~\textrm{A} - 0.173~\textrm{A}</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 0.609~\textrm{A}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
					
					<p>Use Ohm's law to calculate <m>V_X</m>.</p>

					<p>
						<md>
							<mrow>V_X \amp= (0.609~\textrm{A})(20~\Omega)</mrow>
							<mrow>\amp= 12.2~\textrm{V}</mrow>
						</md>
					</p>
				</solution>
			</exercise>
			
		</subexercises>

	</exercises>

</chapter>